[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m If $\det \mathbf{A} = 2$ and $\det \mathbf{B} = 12,$ then find $\det (\mathbf{A} \mathbf{B}).$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m First, recall that for a scalar $\alpha,$ \[\alpha\mathbf{c} = \alpha (\mathbf{c} \cdot \mathbf{c}) (\mathbf{c} \cdot \mathbf{c})^T\] If we write $\mathbf{c} = \mathbf{A} \mathbf{B},$ we can rewrite $\det \mathbf{A}$ and $\det \mathbf{B}$ in terms of the outer product $\mathbf{A} \mathbf{B} = (\mathbf{A} \mathbf{B}, \mathbf{A} \mathbf{B}) = 1 \\ \det \mathbf{C} = \det(\mathbf{A} \mathbf{B} \mathbf{C}).$ Now recall that \[\det(\mathbf{A} \mathbf{C}) = \det \mathbf{A} \det \mathbf{C}\] So $\det \mathbf{B} = \det(\mathbf{A} \mathbf{B} \mathbf{C}) = \det\mathbf{A} \det(\mathbf{B} \mathbf{C}) = 12 \times 2^2 = 48.$
