[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m If the three points $(1,a,b),$ $(a,2,b),$ $(a,b,3)$ are collinear, what is the value of $a + b$?
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To determine the value of \(a + b\) given that the points \((1, a, b)\), \((a, 2, b)\), and \((a, b, 3)\) are collinear, we need to use the concept of the determinant of a matrix formed by these points. A set of three collinear points forms a linear equation in three variables. The determinant of this matrix will be zero.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The points in matrix form are:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \begin{vmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 1 & a & b \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m a & 2 & b \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m a & b & 3
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \end{vmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The area of the triangle formed by these points can be calculated from this determinant. Since all determinant entries are 1 (as they lie in matrix form), we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \begin{vmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 1 & a & b \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m a & 2 & b \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m a & b & 3
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \end{vmatrix} = 1 \cdot \begin{vmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 2 & b \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m b & 3
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \end{vmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Expanding this determinant, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \begin{vmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 2 & b \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m b & 3
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \end{vmatrix} = 2 \cdot 3 - b \cdot b = 6 - b^2
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Thus, the area of the triangle is:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 6 - b^2
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m For the points to be collinear, the area must be zero:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 6 - b^2 = 0
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Solving for \(b^2\), we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m b^2 = 6
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since \(a\) is arbitrary and does not affect the collinearity due to the determinant being zero, the value of \(a + b\) remains the same regardless of the specific value of \(b\). But we need to find the value of \(a + b\) given \(b^2 = 6\).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since \(b\) can be positive or negative depending on \(a \neq 0\), we consider the case when \(b = 0\) to find the minimal \(a\):
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m a^2 = 6 \implies a = \pm \sqrt{6}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The expression \(a + b\) must be minimized, and since \(b = 0\), the smallest value of \(a + b\) when simplified gives us:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m a + b = \sqrt{6} 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since \(a\) and \(b\) are constrained to be real numbers and fixed and \(\sqrt{6} \approx 2.45>0\), the minimum sum \(a + b\) is:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \boxed{2.45}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
