[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To solve the problem, we need to find the integer \( n \) such that \(-90 \le n \le 90\) and \(\sin(n^\circ) = \sin(604^\circ)\). We know from trigonometric properties that the sine function is periodic with a period of \(360^\circ\), and linearly adjusted by being a multiple of \(360^\circ\) (i.e., no changes in the sign).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m First, we reduce \(604^\circ\) modulo \(360^\circ\):
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 604^\circ \div 360^\circ = 1 \text{ remainder } 244^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m So, \(604^\circ = 360^\circ \times 1 + 244^\circ\), indicating that \(604^\circ\) is equivalent to \(244^\circ\).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Next, we need to find an angle \(n^\circ\) such that:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 74^\circ \le n \le 90^\circ
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We use the sine function's periodicity properties. The sine function is symmetric and has a periodicity of \(360^\circ\). Thus, we find the corresponding angle \(n\) to hit the sine value of \(244^\circ\):
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \sin(74^\circ) + \sin(90^\circ - 74^\circ) = \sin(74^\circ) + \sin(16^\circ) = 2 \sin(74^\circ) \cos(-74^\circ)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since \(\cos(-74^\circ) = \cos(74^\circ)\):
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \sin(74^\circ) + \sin(16^\circ) = 2 \sin(74^\circ) \cos(74^\circ)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Now, we use the known values for \(\sin(74^\circ)\) and need to find \(\cos(74^\circ)\). Using trigonometric values or reference angles:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \sin(74^\circ) \approx 0.9613 \quad \text{and} \quad \cos(74^\circ) \approx 0.6691
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Substitute these values into the equation:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 0.9613 + 0.6691 = 1.6304
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m However, this sum does not logically fit with \(\sin(244^\circ) \approx 0.3907\), hinting transformations or integer constraints. Re-evaluate using complex angle application for better control:
