[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Find the volume of the tetrahedron whose vertices are $A = (0,1,2),$ $B = (3,0,1),$ $C = (4,3,6),$ and $D = (2,3,2).$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To find the volume of the tetrahedron with vertices at A = (0,1,2), B = (3,0,1), C = (4,3,6), and D = (2,3,2), we can use the formula for the volume of a tetrahedron in three-dimensional space:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$V = \frac{1}{6} \left| \det \begin{bmatrix} x_1 & y_1 & z_1 & 1 \\ x_2 & y_2 & z_2 & 1 \\ x_3 & y_3 & z_3 & 1 \\ x_4 & y_4 & z_4 & 1 \end{bmatrix} \right|$$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m where (x_1, y_1, z_1) corresponds to point A, (x_2, y_2, z_2) corresponds to point B, (x_3, y_3, z_3) corresponds to point C, and (x_4, y_4, z_4) corresponds to point D.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Calculating the determinant of the matrix has a computational cost of O(m^3), where m = 3. Since the number of dimensions is large, an approximate running time factor is 260.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m However, a more efficient method is to calculate the cross product of each non-null quadruple of vectors formed by each pair of parallel edges. Using these to generate the volume contribution of the tetrahedron, the remaining volume can be reconsidered.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The volume is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$V = \frac{1}{6} \times \frac{6 \times 6 \times 6 \times (12 + 4)}{8+1+1} = \boxed{\frac{1}{8}(6 \times 6 \times 6 \times 16)}= 36 \frac{1}{8}$$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
