[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Yes, a substitution is required. Let $S = \sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac{5n-1}{k^n}.$ Multiplying both sides by $k$ gives
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m kS &= \sum_{n=1}^\infty (5n-1) \cdot \frac{1}{k^n} \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \frac{(k-1) S}{k-1} &= \sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac{5n}{k^n} \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \frac{5}{k}S &= \frac{1}{k}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Using the desired property of geometric series and geometric sum terms, 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[\sum_{n = 1}^{\infty} \frac{x^n}{k^n}= \frac{k}{k-1}. \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Note $\{ S \} =
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$ you realize that $\sum 5n/k^n$ is the same as stretching by 5 times the previously shifted sum, which corresponds to multiplying by $5$ in the previous expression. Thus we get
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$ \frac{\frac{13}{4}}{k-1}=\frac{5}{k} \cdot \frac{\frac{1}{k}}{k-1}$$ which means k = 5. $\blacksquare$
