[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m **Measurement in Base-Cycles:**
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Suppose we want to know how many cycles left out of `182` if they hang out. 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Initially, we have `N` total cycles, stored as an integer value ranging from `0` to max_cycles.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Now, we add (`= +`) some value of the given total
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m If we want to know how many cycles are left out (`= N%max_cycles`),
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m we simply will need to subtract `0` from the initial total.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m **Case 1: Prime Numbers: N=7 and max_cycles=7:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Initially, we have 0 cycles, meaning all the bins are empty.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We will try to fill all the bins by filling the 7th cycle upside, then again the 6th.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m As per the formula `(7+N)/(N+1)`,
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The remaining cycles (`N` - `6 - [Min candidate]`) should be filled by changing the most recently filled bin.
