[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Find $q(x)$ if the graph of $\frac{4x-x^3}{q(x)}$ has a hole at $x=-2$, a vertical asymptote at $x=1$, no horizontal asymptote, and $q(3) = -30$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The original function is $\dfrac{4x - x^3}{q(x)}$. To find the hole at $x = -2$, we can use the fact that $q(x)$ must vanish at $x = -2$, meaning $q(-2) = 0$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m For the vertical asymptote at $x = 1$, the denominator is proportional to $|x - 1|$, which in this case gives us $|x - 1| = 0$, meaning $x - 1 = 0$. This implies $x=1$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m For no horizontal asymptotes, the function approaches $+\infty$ and $-\infty$ as $|x| \to \pm x$, meaning $n(x) \to \infty$ and $-n(x) \to \infty$ as $|x| \to \pm x$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Finally, we need to find $q(3)$, given that $q(3) = -30$. Given the zeros of the quotient, we can multiply the numerator and denominator by $x + 3$ to get:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m q(x) &= 4(x + 3)(x + 2)(x - 1)\\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m &= -2(x + 6)\\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Thus, the function is $q(x) = x + 6$.<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m -3(x+2)(x-1)
