[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To get exactly two CDs in the wrong cases, we need to consider two cases: 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m (a) Two CDs are in (each) wrong case 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m (b) The first CD in a right case is in a wrong case. 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m (We count these cases as distinct items.) 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Case (a) have (1) choices for which (CD) goes to the (wrong) (case), 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m  Case(either (B) or (C), which is (4-cards), so there's (2) choices for (which of the 4) cards go to the (wrong) (case). Thus, the probability of this case is (2). 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Case (b) have (3) choices for which (but) (CD1) goes to a wrong (case), and (2) choices for which (but not) (CD2) goes to a right (case).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Therefore, the probability of this case is 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m [mathmap{0*2+3*2 = 6, proc=word)" (wrong) cards! title 4-cards}][/mathmap]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The probability of case (a) and case (b) combined being correct is therefore (6)/(4 * 4), or (32/16). Hence, the probability that exactly two CDs are in the wrong cases is 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m [mathmap{0*2 / (4) * (32 / (16) 4, proc=word)" (two) bad!) 1(2 / 4 * 4, new=0) = (2 / 4 * 4, proc=word)" (two) bad!)][/mathmap]
