[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m .
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m nants in maths are different from numbers in maths even more than vice-versa, but they are covered most of the time by the 200 year old legacy of the late British mathematician's theory of arithmetic, particularly his "little is very much bigger than big." This is just how 16 is much bigger than 2, when 2 raised to a little number much greater than 1 makes twice as many teeny teeny Canadians after twelve hours.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m .									   $2^{\left(16^x\right)} = 16^{\left(2^x\right)}$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Next spread the exponent in the first equation (the "2"s) across addition "lumping them up": again, this only because the exponents are equalised out! We get $2^{\left(4x\right)} = 16^\left(2x \right)$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m This equation shows up in many other places in mathematics, far apart from arithmetic. It includes logarithms, and differential equations, and finally some real nonetheless interesting real-world capabilities.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $\left(4x\right)/2=2x$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $\left(16^\left(2x\right)\right)/2^{2x}=16^\left(2x\right)\left(1/2\right)^{2x}=2^\left(2x\right)\left(1/2\right)^{2x}=2^{-x}$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Then simply dividing both sides by $2^{-x}$ changes the signs in front (with the safety of zero only at $x=0$). We get
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$2^\left(4x\right)=2^{-x}$$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m etc.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
