[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m My club has 15 members.  In how many ways can we choose a president, vice-president, secretary, and treasurer, if no member can hold more than one office?
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To determine the number of ways to choose the president, vice-president, secretary, and treasurer from 15 members, we can use the concept of permutations. 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The number of permutations of n distinct objects taken r at a time is given by the formula:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m P(r, n) = n! / (n - r)!
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m In this case, n = 15 (the total number of members), and r = 4 (the number of positions to fill). So we are calculating the permutations of 15 members taken 4 at a time:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m P(4, 15) = 15! / (15 - 4)!
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m = 15! / 11!
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Multiplying 15! by 10! gives you all possible combinations of positions, and then dividing through by 11! simplifies the result.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Finally, put the answer within \\boxed{}.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \\boxed{1,210,720}
