[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m It's pretty difficult to give such a concrete example and find a generalized unitary dilogarithm, but it was known that our "unitary dilogarithm" $\mu$ may be expressed as the polynomial $\prod_{s \ge 0, s \text{ odd}} (xr^s + d_x y^s + e_x y^{-s} + f_x x^{-s} + g_x y^{-s} + h_x x^{-s})$ in $x$ and $y,$ $d$, etc. For rational $\mu$, there are some relations that allow us to connect $\mu(x, y)$ with $\pi(x, y)$ in ways that involve only the coefficients of $\prod_{s \ge 0, s \text{ odd}} (xr^s + d_x y^s + e_x y^{-s} + f_x x^{-s} + g_x y^{-s} + h_x x^{-s})$; some of the examples included $\mu(x, y) \equiv \pi(x, y)$ when they are multiplied by a polynomial of odd degree in an adjacent variable $y$.
