[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Find $x$ such that $\log_x 81=\log_2 16$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To solve the problem, we will first convert both logarithms to the same base (e.g., base 2) to make the calculation easier.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Step 1: Convert $\log_x 81$ to base 2.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since $81$ is a power of 3 (i.e., $81 = 3^4$), we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$\log_x 81 = \log_{10} (3^4) = \log_{10} 81$$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Step 2: Convert $\log_2 16$ to base 2.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since $16$ is a power of 2 (i.e., $16 = 2^4$), we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$\log_2 16 = \log_{10} (2^4) = 4 \cdot \log_{10} 2$$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Step 3: Now, set the exponents equal to each other since the logarithmic function with base 2 is monotonic (i.e., it is one-to-one), we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$\log_{10} 81 = 4 \cdot \log_{10} 2$$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Step 4: Solve for $x$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To find $x$, take the antilogarithm (or inverse logarithm) of both sides:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$x = 10^{\log_{10} 81} / 10^4 \cdot 10^{\log_{10} 2} = 180 / 100 \cdot 2 = 3.6$$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The value of $x$ is $\boxed{3.6}$. :)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m .fetchall() can receive an Arrow type implementation via an overloaded operator and fetch it instead of throwing a std::invalid_argument exception and storing as std::vector sorts.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Fetches a single batch of data from the external buffer element manager.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m ::izibility::fetch() calls the trigger_get_new_call() method of the external buffer element manager, which in turn looks for an existing call and get_all(). If the get-all() returned an absent call, it inserts a new call.<|endoftext|>
