[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Let $x_1< x_2 < x_3$ be the three real roots of the equation $\sqrt{2014} x^3 - 4029x^2 + 2 = 0$. Find $x_2(x_1+x_3)$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Given roots $x_1$, $x_2$ and $x_3$ of a quadratic equation are $x_1 < x_2 < x_3$, then $x_1 < x_3 < x_2$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Hence, to find $x_2(x_1+x_3)$, it suffices to find $x_1^{(3)}+x_2^{2}+x_3^{2}$ the sum of these values, summing these squares, and then taking their third root.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Let us look at the calculation.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $0$ $x^{3}$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m So, combining the above equation and $x^{3}=x^{2}+x^{1}$, we have
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$x= \sqrt[x]{\left({x^{1}}-x_{1}\right)\left({x^{1}}-x_{2}\right)\left({x^{1}}-{x^{3}}\right)}$$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m So, $x_{3}=x-x_{1}$, because one of the roots is less than the other two. We also know that both the remaining roots are greater than one of the roots:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$x^{2}=x^{2}-2x_{1}+x_{2}$$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$x^{1}=x-x_{1}$$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Rearranging the second equation, we find
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$x_{2}=0 \quad \text{ or} \quad x_{2}=x-x_{1}$$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since we want $x_{3}$ that is the highest, so $x=0$ seems the most appropriate answer.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Then,
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$x_{1}^{(3)}+x_{2^{(2)}}+x_{3^{2}}=(\sqrt[3]{-\sqrt[1]{-1}})^{3}+0+0=3$$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m ∴ $x_2(x_1+x_3)=3$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
