[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The given expression is $\lceil y \rceil+y=15.5$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m First, find the maximum possible value for $\lceil y \rceil$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The ceiling function rounds a number up towards the next highest integer.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Given $\lceil y \rceil$ $\leq$ $15.5$ becomes slightly less than $15.5$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Therefore, the maximum possible value for $\lceil y \rceil$ is $\lceil 15.5 \rceil = 16$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Next, substitute this value back into the given expression and solve for $y$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $y + 16 = 15.5$ becomes a linear equation with one unknown $y$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The solution is simply the difference between the two values multiplied by one ($y\pi$), which gives $1y = y\pi = y(1 + \pi) = 15.5$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Solving for $y$ gives $y = \frac{15.5}{1(1 + \pi)} = \boxed{3}$.eousystem
