[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m A spherical soap bubble lands on a horizontal wet surface and forms a hemisphere of the same volume. Given the radius of the hemisphere is $3\sqrt[3]{2}$ cm, find the radius of the original bubble.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To determine the radius of the original spherical soap bubble, we start by understanding that the volume of a hemisphere is half the volume of a sphere with the same radius. The volume of a sphere is given by the formula:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ V = \frac{4}{3} \pi r^3 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Given that the volume of the hemisphere is \(\frac{1}{2}V\), we can write:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ \frac{4}{3} \pi r^3 = \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{4}{3} \pi r^3 \right) \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The \(\frac{4}{3}\pi\) terms cancel out on both sides:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ r^3 = \frac{1}{2} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ r = \left( \frac{1}{2} \right)^{1/3} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ r = \frac{1}{\sqrt[3]{2}} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ r = \sqrt[3]{\frac{1}{2}} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m So, the radius of the original spherical bubble is \(\boxed{\sqrt[3]{\frac{1}{2}}}\).<|endoftext|>
