[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    - Hence, the factorization of the product ( n )( n+1 )( n+2 )( n+3) includes \(n\).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 2. **GCD Requirement:**
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    - Since \(n\) must divide the expression, we need a number that includes \(n\) in its prime factorization but not higher powers than \(n\).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 3. **Strategy:**
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    - Consider the difference divide: \(n\) minus any integer divides.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    - One straightforward estimator is \(n - 256\).
