[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Xanthia reads 100 pages per hour, so in one hour, she reads:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \(100 \text{ pages/hour} \times 1 \text{ hour} = 100 \text{ pages}\)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Molly reads 50 pages per hour, which also works out to 100 pages per hour, so in one hour, she reads:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \(100 \text{ pages/hour} \times 1 \text{ hour} = 100 \text{ pages}\)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Step 2: Calculate the total number of pages completely read by each.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since the book has 225 pages and both read at the same rate, the total number of pages read by Xanthia is 225 pages, and the total number of pages read by Molly is 225 pages.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Step 3: Find the difference in the number of pages read by both.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The difference in the number of pages read is:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \(225 \text{ pages} - 225 \text{ pages} = 0 \text{ pages}\)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Step 4: Convert the unknown time (in minutes) to hours to calculate the difference in reading time.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since the book only has 225 pages, it's 225 pages and not a reading time problem or a specific reading speed problem. We are looking for the time difference in hours, not pages.
