[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \(\theta = c\) is the polar coordinate mentioned. The equation of a sphere in spherical coordinates \((\rho,\theta,\phi)\) is \(\rho\cos\theta = c\). Here, \(\rho\) is the radial distance, \(\theta\) is the polar angle, and \(\phi\) is the azimuthal angle. So, the equation of a sphere is:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[\rho = \frac{c}{\cos\theta}.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Now, for the equation \(\theta = c\), we substitute \(\theta = c\) into \(\rho = \frac{c}{\cos\theta}\):
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[\rho = \frac{c}{\cos(c)}.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m This is a surface passing through the point \((0, \cos(c), c)\), which is the point \((0, r_c, c)\) of the surface of 2D, \(\rho = \frac{c}{\cos(c)}\), when \((\rho, \theta, \phi) = (0, c, \text{some } \phi)\) and \((\rho, \theta, \phi) = (r_c, \text{some } \phi, \text{some } \phi_r)\).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m This represents a cylinder if the initial equation is written in the form \(\rho = \frac{c}{\cos\theta}\) with \(0 \leq\theta \leq \frac{\pi}{2}\).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Therefore, the shape of the equation \(\theta = c\) on \((\rho,\theta,\phi)\) is a **Cylinder**.
