[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Calculate $11m + n$ where $m$ is our greatest multiple of $11$ and $11n$ is our smallest multiple of $11$. Let $x$ be that number and $y$ be that number subtracted off from $x$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Calculate $11m + n$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Turn the number $11$ ($m$) into an equation again to find $m$. The smallest multiple of $11$ greater than $1000$ may be written as $x$. 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m This means we want to include $m$ in the denominator of the fraction we get by dividing $m$ by $11$ - say $1$ - and also $1000$ subtracted off from the value we get.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Example: 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $\frac{1850}{11}$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $= 182 + 31$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $\frac{121}{11}$ = $11 + 1$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Turn the number $11$ ($m$) into a fraction of itself by multiplying it by $11$ - in the case where $m$ is the least multiple less than $1$, your number is $1$, so you need to add $1$ to it:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $= 1 + 1$
