[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - \( \text{Circle centered at } O \) with radius 1.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - \( \angle AOB = \theta \).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - \( \text{Segment } AB \) is tangent to the circle at point } A. \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - \( \angle AOB = \theta \).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - Point \( C \) lies on \( \overline{OA} \).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - \( \overline{BC} \) bisects \( \angle ABO \).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Let's label the points and lines:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 1. \( \text{Circle: } O \) (origin)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 2. \( C \) is at \( (0.6, 0) \).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 3. \( A \) is at \( (1, 0) \).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 4. \( \text{Ray } AO \) is the radius.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 5. \( \text{Ray } BO \) is the tangent at \( A \).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 6. \( \overline{BC} \) bisects \( \angle ABO \).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m First, let's determine the coordinates of \( B \). Since \( AB \) is tangent to the circle at \( A \), the line segment \( BO \) is perpendicular to \( AB \). The slope of \( AO \) is undefined (vertical line), so the slope of \( BO \) must be infinite (vertical line) as well. Hence, \( B \) is also at \( (1, 0) \).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Now, let's consider the length \( OC \):
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 1. \( C \) lies on \( \overline{OA} \), so \( C \) has coordinates \( (a, 0) \) where \( a \) is between 0 and 1.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 2. Since \( \overline{BC} \) bisects \( \angle ABO \), it means \( C \) must be at a point such that the tangent at \( B \) intersects \( \overline{OC} \) at the midpoint.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To find \( a \):
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - The tangent at \( B \) will touch \( AB \).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - The length of \( AB \) is 1 unit (since \( A \) to \( B \) lies along the tangent at \( B \) and the radius of the circle makes it a right triangle).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - The distance \( AB \) is simply the radius of the circle, so \( AC = 1 - a \) because \( C \) is farther along \( \overline{OA} \) than \( A \).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Given \( \angle AOB = \theta \):
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - During the angle bisector at \( A \), the \( \angle ABO = \theta \), implying \( \angle CBO = \frac{\theta}{2} \).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - Since \( C \) is at \( (a, 0) \) and \( \overline{BO} \) is horizontal with \( C \) above \( A \), the coordinates of \( B \) must be such that the angle at \( C \) in the triangle \( AOB \) is consistent with the sine and cosine angles applied to these points.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Hence:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - \( OC \)'s length can be calculated utilizing the sine and cosine of the angle at \( C \).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Thus:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m OC = 1 - a
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Given \( s = \sin \theta \):
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \text{sine}(\theta) = \frac{a}{1} = s \Rightarrow a = s
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Then:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m OC = 1 - s
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Thus, the final answer is:
