[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Ioana has three ropes whose lengths are 39 inches, 52 inches and 65 inches. She wants to cut the ropes into equal length pieces for magic tricks. No rope is to be wasted. What is the greatest number of inches possible in the length of each piece?
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To find the greatest possible length for each piece, we need to find the least common multiple (LCM) of the rope lengths, and then divide each length by that LCM.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The lengths of the rope are 39 inches, 52 inches, and 65 inches.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The LCM of 39, 52, and 65 is 3670. This is because the LCM can be found by finding the prime factors of each number and taking the highest power of each prime factor that appears in the factorization. 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 39 = 3 x 13
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 52 = 2^2 x 13
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 65 = 5 x 13
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m So the LCM is 2^2 x 3 x 13 = 132 inches.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Now divide each rope length by the LCM to find the possible piece lengths:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Piece 1: 39 inches / 132 inches = 3/8 inches
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Piece 2: 52 inches / 132 inches = 13/30 inches
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Piece 3: 65 inches / 132 inches = 23/33 inches
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To get the greatest piece length, we can take the average of the three piece lengths:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m (3/8 + 13/30 + 23/33) / 3 = (33/360 + 52/360 + 72/360) / 3 = (109/360) / 3 = 109/1080 inches
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m So the greatest possible length for each piece is 109/1080 inches. \boxed{109/1080}<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 13
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
