[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Four people can mow a lawn in 6 hours. How many more people will be needed to mow the lawn in 4 hours, assuming each person mows at the same rate?
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Sure, let's analyze this step by step. We first need to determine the rate at which the mowers work together to mow the lawn. If 4 people can mow a lawn in 6 hours, then the total number of hours to mow the lawn is \(1\) hour (since 1 lawn per division). So, one person mowing can cut \(6 \div 1 = 6\) lines in 6 hours. Adding the work of 4 people, the total rate is \(6 \text{ lines/hour} \times 4 = 24\) lines/hour.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Now we want to figure out how many more people will be needed to mow the lawn in 4 hours if we had \(x\) people working. Using the total rate of 24 lines/hour, with \(x\) people working at the new rate \(24x\) lines/hour, we set this equal to \(24\). Therefore, \(24x = 24\), and solving for \(x\) gives:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m x = \frac{24}{24} = 1
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m So, four people will be needed to mow the lawn in 4 hours when they are working at the same rate. Putting it all together, the answer is \( \boxed{1} \).<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
