[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m At breakfast, lunch, and dinner, Joe randomly chooses with equal probabilities either an apple, an orange, or a banana to eat. On a given day, what is the probability that Joe will eat at least two different kinds of fruit?
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To calculate the probability that Joe will eat at least two different kinds of fruit, we need to consider the different scenarios in which he can achieve this.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The possible scenarios are:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 1. Eating at least two different kinds of fruit on the day
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 2. Eating all different kinds of fruit on the day
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 3. All different kinds of fruit on only one day
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Scenario 1: Eating at least two different kinds of fruit
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m In this scenario, there are 5 possible scenarios to eate fruit: apple, orange, apple, orange, banana. There are \frac{{PC({8})}}{{PC({8 + 12})}}= rounded to two decimal places= 0.750000=0.75 different kinds of fruit, 1, 2, 3, 4, or 5. If Joe eats at least two different types of fruit on the day, the probability is 0.50 x 1 = 50%= 0.5
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Scenario 2: Eating all different kinds of fruit on only one day
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We need to determine the probability of Joe not eating at least two different kinds of fruit while eating all different kinds of fruit. Joe can choose between having different kinds of fruit twice, so the possible combinations are 5 x 4 = 20. In 20%=rounded to two decimal places=0.20 different combinations, he won't eat at least two different kinds of fruit, and therefore, the probability is 0.2
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Scenario 3: Eating all different kinds of fruit on only one day
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Following the same logic as Scenario 2, Joe can choose between having different kinds of fruit only once, so the possible combinations are 5 x 4 = 20. In 20%=rounded to two decimal places=0.20 different combinations, he won't eat at least two different kinds of fruit, and therefore, the probability is 0.2
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m So the probability that Joe will eat at least two different kinds of fruit is 0.5 + 0.75 + 0.2 = 1.45 = 0.85
