[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Easy enough. Here’s a way to tackle the problem. \[\sin x = \left( \frac{1}{2} \right)^x\] can be rewritten as $$\sin x = (-2)^{\frac{-1}{i}}.$$ So we have $$s_{i-1}+(-2)^{\frac{-\cdot1}{i-1}}=\frac{\pi}{2}+\frac{i\pi}{2}.$$ If $i=0,$ this means we only need to look at $x\in[0,\frac{\pi}{6}).$ If $i=1,$ we need to look at $x\in[\frac{\pi}{6},\frac{\pi}{12}).$ Currently, when I read this problem I suggest thinking of the sine function as being intersected at $(0,1)$. If you’re giving a number as input delimited by a “|”, that will instantly make you read it as an integer ($\in i$), instead of a boundary at $\frac{1}{2}.$
