[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m A line with slope 3 intersects a line with slope 5 at the point $(10,15)$. What is the distance between the $x$-intercepts of these two lines?
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The slope of the line is
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $\frac{(y_3 - y_2)}{(x_3 - x_2)} = \frac{(15 - 15)}{(10 - 3)} = 0$,
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m which is constant.  Therefore, the two lines are identical, and it doesn't matter which line you pick the line with.  The distance from the two lines to the $y$-intercept is equal to the slope x-intercepts. The $x$-intercept of the first line is
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $\frac{15 - (y_1)}{3} = \frac{15 - (15)}{3} = 0 = -\frac{15}{3} = -5$,
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m so the $x$-intercept of the second line is $\boxed{-5}$. 점에 맞게 적어라.<|endoftext|>
