[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Eleven rectangular 9-inch by 12-inch pictures hang on a wall. How many square inches of the wall's surface area are covered by the pictures?
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Step 1: Calculate the surface 9-inch of each picture. The area of the rectangle is calculated by multiplying its length and width, so in this case:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Area = 9 inches * 12 inches = 108 square inches for each picture.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Step 2: Calculate the total covering 9-inch of each picture together:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Total area = Area per picture * Number of pictures = 108 square inches * 11 pictures = 1188 square inches.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Step 3: Since all pictures are of the same size, we can calculate the total area of one rectangle of a single wall (12-inch by 9-inch area). 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m For one medium wall (12-inch x 9-inch size), the total area is calculated by multiplying its length and width, so it will be 12 inches * 9 inches = 108 square inches. 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Step 4: Calculate the total covering for all the walls. The total covering area combined would be total area of one wall * Number of rows and columns:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Total covering = 108 square inches * Number of rows and columns. 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m If there are n rows, each row will cover 108 / n square inches. So total uncovered area for n rows is 108 / n square inches.
