[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To determine the area between the graph of $y = 3f(x - 2)$ and the $x$-axis, we need to understand how the transformation affects the original function $y = f(x)$. The transformation $y = 3f(x - 2)$ involves a horizontal shift of the graph of $y = f(x)$ horizontally by 2 units to the right, and a vertical compression by a factor of 3.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Here are the steps to find the area:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 1. **Identify the vertical compression factor**: The factor of 3 in front of $f(x)$ indicates a horizontal compression by a factor of 3. This means we need to multiply the area under the graph of $y = f(x)$ by $3^2 = 9$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 2. **Understand the effect of the horizontal shift**: The function $y = f(x - 2)$ shifts the graph of $y = f(x)$ to the right by 2 units. This means we need to subtract the area under the graph of $y = f(x - 2)$ from the area under the graph of $y = f(x)$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 3. **Calculate the area**:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \text{Area under } y = f(x) \text{ before the shift: } 10 \text{ square units}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \text{Area under } y = 3f(x - 2) \text{ before the shift: } 3 \times (10) = 30 \text{ square units}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \text{Area between } y = f(x) \text{ and } y = 3f(x - 2) \text{ with the shift: } 30 - 10 = 20 \text{ square units}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Therefore, the area between the graph of $y = 3f(x - 2)$ and the $x$-axis is \boxed{20} square units.<|endoftext|>
