[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m When applying the long division method, we start with an unambiguous denominator and then look for a way to divide the numerator by it. You've started with a literal division from the start! So you have to divide the nominative root by the denominator: $\dfrac{11}{125} \div \dfrac{1}{5} = \underline{11} \div \underline{5}$ The digit at the top (1) is greater than the digit at the base (5). It would (always) divide forever to the right. Twenty-four continues until we have to go back to a base ten division. So what is the remainder? Well $125$ fits in our 24, since $\dfrac{11}{24} = 0,4166...$ So 11 is in the $125$ ad indefinitely, then $5$ are in the $125^{24}$ ad!\boxed{{}\cdot 8^1 \cdot 125^{124}}<|endoftext|>
