[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The volume of a cube is 0.027 cubic meters. What is its surface area in square meters?
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We can find the surface area of the cube by finding the length of one of its sides and then using it to calculate areas of the top and bottom faces and two lateral faces.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The volume \( V \) of a cube with side length \( a \) is given by the equation \( V = a^3 \). We are given that the volume \( V \) is 0.027 cubic meters. Therefore,
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m a^3 = 0.027
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Taking the cube root of both sides, we find the side length \( a \):
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m a = \sqrt[3]{0.027} \approx 0.3 = 3 \text{ meters}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The surface area \( A \) of a cube is given by the formula \( A = 6a^2 \). Substituting \( a = 3 \) meters into the formula, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m A = 6 \times (3)^2 = 6 \times 9 = 54 \text{ square meters}
