[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m From the given equation $\operatorname{Tr} (\mathbf{A}^2) = 30,\ \ \ $ we can infer that  \begin{align} \mathbf{A}^2 &= (\mathbf{A}) (\mathbf{A}) \\ \mathbf{A}^2 &= \mathbf{A} (\mathbf{A}) \\ \mathbf{A}^2 &= \mathbf{A} (\mathbf{A}) \end{align} Using the commutative property of multiplication, we can rewrite the equation $\operatorname{Tr} (\mathbf{A}^2) = 30$ as \begin{align}  \operatorname{Tr} \mathbf{A}^2 &= 30 \\ \mathbf{A} (\mathbf{A}) &= 30 \end{align} Now,  $\mathbf{A}$ is an $n\times n$ matrix, so $\mathbf{A}$ has an $n$-th root.  The $n$-th root of a matrix $\mathbf{A}$ is defined as  \begin{align} \mathbf{A}^{n/2} &= \left( \mathbf{A}^{\frac{1}{2}} \right)^n \\ \left( \mathbf{A}^{\frac{1}{2}} \right)^n &= \left( \mathbf{A} \right) ^{\frac{n}{2}} \end{align} Note that $\mathbf{A}^{n/2}$ is an $n/2$-th root of $\mathbf{A}, \ \ \ $and  \begin{align} \mathbf{A}^{n/2} &= \left( \sqrt[n]{\mathbf{A}} \right)^n \\ \left( \mathbf{A}^{n/2} \right)^2 &= (\sqrt[n]{\mathbf{A}})^n \\ (\mathbf{A}^{n/2})^2 &= \mathbf{A}^{n/2} \end{align}  Thus we may rewrite  $\operatorname{Tr} \mathbf{A}^2 = 30$ as \begin{align} \left( \mathbf{A}^{n/2} \right)^2 &= \mathbf{A}^{n/2} \\ \left( \mathbf{A}^{n/2} \right)^2 &= \mathbf{A}^{n/2}. \end{align} Equating the $n$-th powers of the two sides, we find $\mathbf{A}^{n} = \mathbf{A}.$  Finally,  $\mathbf{A} \mathbf{I} = \mathbf{A},$ so we conclude that  $\mathbf{A}$ is an idempotent matrix, where  $ \mathbf{I} $ is a $n\times n$ identity matrix.  In particular,  $\mathbf{A}$ is a symmetric matrix, where  $\mathbf{A} = \mathbf{A}^T.$  Lastly, since $\mathbf{A} \mathbf{A}^T = \mathbf{A}^T \mathbf{A} = \mathbf{A},$ we know that  $ \mathbf{A}^T = \mathbf{A}.$  So,  $\mathbf{A} = \mathbf{A}^{T} = \mathbf{A}.$ Now,  $\mathbf{A} = \mathbf{A}^T.$ Once we know this,  $\mathbf{A}^3 = \mathbf{A} \mathbf{A} \mathbf{A} = \mathbf{A}.$ Since $\mathbf{A}$ is a symmetric,  $\mathbf{A}^3 = \mathbf{A}. \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ WLOG, \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ A = \begin{pmatrix} a & b \\ c & d \end{pmatrix},$ T H E K K  So  \begin{align} \mathbf{A}^3 &= \mathbf{A} \\ \mathbf{A}^3 &= A \\ (A)^3 &= A \\ A^3& = A. \end{align} So,  $\mathbf{A} = \begin{pmatrix} a & b \\ c & d \end{pmatrix}$ is the unique symmetric idempotent matrix with trace equal to the product of the diagonal elements.  Thus  $\det \mathbf{A} = \operatorname{Tr} (\mathbf{A}) =  \begin{vmatrix} a & b \\ c & d \end{vmatrix} = ad - bc = 3 \cdot 12 = \boxed{36}.e %=
