[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Yes, here is a step-by-step solution.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m ıklwe reversed the repeating fractional part and concatenated the digits together. The result is $abc$; we can denote this number by $\overline{abc}$. Since $0<y\le9$, $\overline{abc}$ has only 1 digit between the strings $scancb$ and $cba$, and it may be that $a,c$ are 0, in which case $\overline{abc}$ only has 1 digit.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m By the same, we know that $0<y\le9$ for $s$ and also for $l$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To figure out the largest value of $\overline{abc}$, we need to figure out the largest value of $l$ among those $l$ keeping it between $0$ and $9$, because when $l = 0$, the value of $\overline{abc}$ would become just a single digit.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m rewriting $l$ as $y=$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m if $y=(l+1)d$, then $\overline{abc}+\overline{l\d}=\overline{d+ld}=10dll=10(l+y)=gcd(l,y)=(l,1)=1$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m if $l=y$, then $\overline{abc}=\overline{l}$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Small word of caution: in this question we're actually lying; $\overline{abc}$ is drawing 1 out of 10 methods to place digits for the whole division of 1 back to 1; there are other trivial cycle - making the division impossible.
