[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To convert a point from cylindrical coordinates (\( (r, \theta, z) \)) to rectangular coordinates (\( (x, y, z) \)), we use the following formulas:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m x = r \cos(\theta)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m y = r \sin(\theta)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m z = z
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Given:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m (r, \theta, z) = \left( 8, \frac{\pi}{4}, \sqrt{3} \right)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Plug in the values and solve for \( x \), \( y \), and \( z \):
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m x = 8 \cos\left( \frac{\pi}{4} \right)
