[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m In a 5 by 5 grid, each of the 25 small squares measures 2 cm by 2 cm and is shaded. Five unshaded circles are then placed on top of the grid as shown. The area of the visible shaded region can be written in the form $A-B\pi$ square cm. What is the value $A+B$?
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m [asy]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m for(int i = 0; i < 5; ++i)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m {
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m for(int j = 0; j < 5; ++j)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m {
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m filldraw((i,j)--(i+1,j)--(i+1,j+1)--(i,j+1)--(i,j)--cycle,gray,linewidth(2));
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m }
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m }
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m filldraw(circle((2.5,.5),.5),white,linewidth(2));
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m filldraw(circle((4.5,2.5),.5),white,linewidth(2));
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m filldraw(circle((2.5,4.5),.5),white,linewidth(2));
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m filldraw(circle((.5,2.5),.5),white,linewidth(2));
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m filldraw(circle((2.5,2.5),1.5),white,linewidth(2));
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m [/asy]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To solve this problem, we need to calculate the area of the visible shaded region after placing five unshaded circles on top of a 5 by 5 grid.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 1. **Calculate the total shaded area of the circles**:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    - Each circle has an area of \( \pi \cdot 1^2 = \pi \) square cm.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    - There are 5 circles, so the total shaded area is:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m      \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m      5 \times \pi = 5\pi \text{ square cm}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m      \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 2. **Calculate the area of the unshaded regions**:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    - Each small square in the grid has an area of \( 2 \cdot 2 = 4 \) square cm.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    - The effective area of the grid is reduced by the area of the circles plus the average area of the surrounding squares.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    - There are \( 25 - 5 = 20 \) grids in the grid.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    - The perimeter of each grid is \( 2 \times 4 = 8 \) cm.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    - The side length of each grid area is \( \sqrt{8} \approx 2.83 \) cm.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    - The average area of the surrounding squares within each grid is approximately \( \frac{8 \times 4}{20} \times 4 = 16 \) square cm.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 3. **Calculate the effective shaded area of the grid**:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    - The effective area of each grid is reduced by the total area of the circles:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m      \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m      \text{Effective shaded area of each grid} = 16 - 5\pi
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m      \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    - The total effective shaded area for all grids is:
