[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To solve $2^{16} \div 16^2$, we start by simplifying the exponents. We know that $16^2 = (2^4)^2 = 2^8$ since squaring any number gives us its square. Now the original expression becomes $2^{16} \div 2^8$. Using the properties of exponents, specifically division of powers, we have $(2^{16} \div 2^8) = 2^{16 - 8} = 2^8$. Therefore, $2^{16} \div 16^2 = 2^8 = 256$. $\boxed{256}$<|endoftext|>
