[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To find the angles \(\lambda_1\) and \(\lambda_2\), we need to use the work-energy principle for the rotating rod. The total rotational kinetic energy of the rod is conserved because there are no external torques acting on the rod (assuming the rod is long and thin without any significant mass distribution).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The total rotational kinetic energy \(K\) of the rod when it rotates with angular acceleration \(\alpha\) is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ K = \frac{1}{2} I \omega^2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m where \(I\) is the moment of inertia of the rod and \(\omega\) is the angular velocity.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m First, we need to find the moment of inertia \(I\) of the rod rotating about one end. The moment of inertia for a uniform slender rod about one end is \(\frac{1}{3}ML^2\), where \(M\) is the mass of the rod and \(L\) is the length of the rod.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The rod rotates downward as it passes through one end with angle \(\theta\). The length of this segment of the rod is \(\frac{L}{4}\). The angular velocity \(\omega\) at this point is \(\omega_1 = \frac{dL}{dt} \cdot \frac{I_R}{L} = \alpha \frac{L}{4} \cdot \frac{I_{rot}}{L} = \alpha \frac{L}{4} \cdot I\), where \(I_{rot}\) is the moment of inertia of a point mass at the intersection of the rod and the axis of rotation.
