[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To find the greatest number of interior right angles a convex octagon can have, we need to understand the properties of convex polygons.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m A convex octagon is a polygon with 8 sides and its interior angles should add up to (8-2)*180° = 1080°. To determine any possible number of interior angles (an modulus), it's important to find the restriction. To avoid having a 28-degree angle, the 1080° must be divisible by 361. However, not all over-divisible by 361 give a convex octagon.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Remember that a convex octagon can't have exactly the same angle on both sides of the axis (caused by its symmetry), and it must have at least one interior angle. The only balance that does not cause division by zero is the number of interior angles having a quadrant, which results in 360°/4 = 90° each. Including these 360°, the sum of the interior angles is 900°.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 360° - the final unqualified 90° angle we want
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
