[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To rationalize the denominator, we will multiply both the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The conjugate of $$\sqrt{108}+2\sqrt{12}+2\sqrt{27}$$ is $$\sqrt{108}-2\sqrt{12}+2\sqrt{27}$$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Multiplying the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate, we get $$\frac{4}{\sqrt{108}+2\sqrt{12}+2\sqrt{27}} \cdot \frac{\sqrt{108}-2\sqrt{12}+2\sqrt{27}}{\sqrt{108}-2\sqrt{12}+2\sqrt{27}}$$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m This simplifies to $$\frac{4(\sqrt{108}-2\sqrt{12}+2\sqrt{27})}{(108+2\cdot 2\sqrt{12}+2\cdot 2\sqrt{27})(\sqrt{108}-2\sqrt{12}+2\sqrt{27})}$$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Simplifying further, we get $$\frac{4(\sqrt{108}-2\sqrt{12}+2\sqrt{27})}{(108+4\cdot 2\sqrt{12}+4\cdot 2\sqrt{27})(\sqrt{108}-2\sqrt{12}+2\sqrt{27})}$$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The denominator is now a difference of squares, which simplifies to $$(\sqrt{108})^2 - 2(2\sqrt{12})(2\sqrt{27}) + (2\sqrt{27})^2$$, which equals $$(63)-48+108$$, and equals $53$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Simplifying the numerator, we get $$4(\sqrt{108}-2\sqrt{12}+2\sqrt{27})=4(2\sqrt{6}-4\sqrt{3}+2\sqrt{3})=4(\sqrt{6}-2\sqrt{3})$$, and simplifying the fraction further, we get $$\frac{4(\sqrt{6}-2\sqrt{3})}{53}$$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m So the final answer is $$\frac{4(\sqrt{6}-2\sqrt{3})}{53}$$.flater<|endoftext|>
