[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $\mathbf{u} \times \mathbf{v} + \mathbf{u} = \mathbf{w}$ means that $\mathbf{u} = \mathbf{w} - \mathbf{v} - \mathbf{u} \times \mathbf{v}$ and the same applies to $\mathbf{u} \times \mathbf{w}$ and $\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{w}.$ That is, the result of two scalar-multiplication equations is the same as a scalar-multiplication equation resulting from one of the equations. Continuing to work the elements of the above equations, we get $\mathbf{u} = - \mathbf{u} + \mathbf{v}.$ Because the elements of the left and right hands of this equation are always equal, we must have that $\mathbf{u} = -\mathbf{u} + \mathbf{v} - \mathbf{v} = - 2 \mathbf{u}.$ Therefore, $\mathbf{u} = -2 \mathbf{u}$ and must be the zero vector.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Now we can form our final answer and compute $\mathbf{u} \cdot (\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{w})$. \mathbf{u} \cdot (\mathbf{u} + \mathbf{v} + \mathbf{w}) \mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{w} = \mathbf{u} \cdot (-2 \mathbf{u} + \mathbf{v} + \mathbf{w}) $\mathbf{u} \cdot (-2 \mathbf{u}) + \mathbf{u} \cdot \mathbf{v} + \mathbf{u} \cdot \mathbf{w} = - 2 \mathbf{u} \cdot \mathbf{u} + \mathbf{u} \cdot \mathbf{v} + \mathbf{u} \cdot \mathbf{w} \mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{u} + \mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{v} + \mathbf{w} \cdot \mathbf{v} + \mathbf{w} \cdot \mathbf{u} = - 2 \mathbf{u} \cdot \mathbf{u} + 0 + 0 = - 2 \mathbf{u} \cdot \mathbf{u}$ \boxed{}\mathbf{u} \cdot (\mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{w}) = -2 \mathbf{u} \cdot \mathbf{u} equity
