[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Calculate the concentration of NaCl in a saturated solution after a 38 g sample of pure hydrochloric acid (HCl) is dissolved in 500 g of water at 20 °C. To calculate the concentration of NaCl in a saturated solution of HCl after a 38 g sample of pure HCl is dissolved in 500 g of water at 20 °C, we can follow these steps:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 1. **Determine the molar volume of HCl:**
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    The molar volume of HCl at 20 °C is approximately 17.8 g/mol.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 2. **Calculate the mass of NaCl in the saturated solution:**
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    Since the solutes dissociate, NaCl always dissociates into Na\(^+\) and Cl\(^-\) in solution.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \text{Mass of NaCl} = \text{Molar mass of NaCl} = \text{Mass of NaCl} \times \text{Concentration of NaCl} = \left(\frac{\text{mass of NaCl}}{\text{concentration of NaCl}}\right)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    Given:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \text{Concentration of NaCl} = \frac{\text{mass of NaCl}}{\text{mass of saturated solution}} = \frac{38 \text{ g}}{500 \text{ g}}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \text{Concentration of NaCl} = 0.076 \text{ M}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    Now, calculate the mass of NaCl in the saturated solution:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \text{Mass of NaCl} = \text{Concentration of NaCl} \times \text{Mass of saturated solution} = 0.076 \text{ M} \times 500 \text{ g} = 38 \text{ g}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 3. **Calculate the total mass of the saturated solution:**
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \text{Total mass of the saturated solution} = \text{mass of HCl} + \text{mass of NaCl} = 38 \text{ g} + 38 \text{ g} = 76 \text{ g}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 4. **Determine the concentration of NaCl in the saturated solution:**
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    The volume of the saturated solution is 500 g. Use the molar concentration to find the concentration of NaCl:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \text{Concentration of NaCl} = \frac{\text{mass of NaCl}}{\text{Total mass of the solution}} = \frac{38 \text{ g}}{500 \text{ g}}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \text{Concentration of NaCl} = 0.076 \text{ M}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Thus, the concentration of NaCl in the saturated solution is \boxed{0.076} M.<|endoftext|>
