[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To convert \((r, \theta)\) from polar coordinates to rectangular coordinates, we use the formulas:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m x = r \cos(\theta), \quad y = r \sin(\theta)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Given:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m r = 2 \sqrt{3}, \quad \theta = \frac{2 \pi}{3}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m First, we need to find \(\cos(\frac{2 \pi}{3})\) and \(\sin(\frac{2 \pi}{3})\). (Note that \(\frac{2 \pi}{3}\) is an angle in degrees.)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The cosine of \(\frac{2 \pi}{3}\) is \(-\frac{1}{2}\) and the sine of \(\frac{2 \pi}{3}\) is \(\frac{1}{2}\).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Now we can calculate the rectangular coordinates:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m x = 2 \sqrt{3} \cos\left(\frac{2 \pi}{3}\right) = 2 \sqrt{3} \left(-\frac{1}{2}\right) = -\sqrt{3}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m y = 2 \sqrt{3} \sin\left(\frac{2 \pi}{3}\right) = 2 \sqrt{3} \left(\frac{1}{2}\right) = \sqrt{3}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m So, the rectangular coordinates are \(( -\sqrt{3}, \sqrt{3} )\).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m boxed{
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \begin{aligned} x &=  -\sqrt{3} \\ y &= \sqrt{3} \end{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m }<|endoftext|>
