[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To find the expected value of the roll of an octahedral die, we need to calculate the average outcome when the die is rolled repeatedly. For a fair octahedral die, the expected value can be calculated using the following formula: Expected value = (first outcome points × number of faces) + (second outcome points × number of faces) + (third outcome points × number of faces) + ... + (last outcome points × number of faces).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Both sides of this formula are simple additions of individual outcomes, so we just need to multiply one face by the total number of faces. The formula can be rewritten as:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Expected value = (1 × 8) + (2 × 8) + (3 × 8) + (4 × 8) + (5 × 8) + (6 × 8) + (7 × 8) + (8 × 8)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m simplified as:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Expected value = (48)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Thus, the expected value for a fair octahedral die is (48)._putchar<|endoftext|>
