[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Line $a$ is parallel to line $b$ and goes through the point $(1,2)$. Line $b$ goes through the point $(3,1)$ and is perpendicular to line $c$ whose equation is $y=-2x+3$. Find the y-intercept of line $a$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The equation of line $a$ can be written as $y - 2 = m(x - 1)$, where $m$ is the slope.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since line $b$ is perpendicular to line $c$, the slope of line $b$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope of line $c$. The slope of line $c$ is $-2$, so the slope of line $b$ is $\dfrac{1}{2}$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since line $a$ is parallel to line $b$, they must have the same slope. Thus, the slope of line $a$ is also $m = \dfrac{1}{2}$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Using the point $b(3,1)$, we can find the y-intercept of line $a$ using the equation $y - 1 = \dfrac{1}{2}(x - 3)$. This gives us $y = \dfrac{1}{2}x - 2.5$. The y-intercept is therefore $-2.5$.boxed{}<|endoftext|>
