[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We want to find the first three digits to the right of the decimal point in $\left(10^{2002}+1\right)^{10/7}$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To do this, we can first write the expression in the form $(a+1)^{10/7}$, where $a=10^{2002}$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Next, we can simplified the power as follows:$$\left(10^{2002}+1\right)^{10/7}=\left(10^{2002}+1\right)^{10/7}\left(10^{2002}+1\right)^{7/70}.$$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since $10^{7/70}=\left(10^{2002}+1\right)^{7/69}$, the final expression is rewritten as $\left(10^{2002}+1\right)^{10/7}\left(10^{2002}+1\right)^{7/69}$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We can then apply the binomial theorem to simplify the expression:$$\left(10^{2002}+1\right)^{10/7}\left(10^{2002}+1\right)^{7/69}=\sum_{k=0}^{10/7} \binom{10/7}{k} 10^{10k/7}=\sum_{k=0}^{10/7} \binom{10/7}{k} 10^k,$$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m where $\binom{n}{k}=\frac{n(n-1)(n-2)\dots(n-k+1)}{k!}$ is the binomial coefficient.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To find the first digit to the right of the decimal point in the decimal representation of $\left(10^{2002}+1\right)^{10/7}$, we can ignore the leading zeroes and observe the first three digits to the right of the exponent.
