[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m What is the area, in square units, of a square whose sides are the same length as the radius of a circle with a circumference of $12\pi$ units?
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To find the area of the square, we need to determine the radius of the circle. The circumference of a circle is given by the formula:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Circumference = 2\pi \times \text{radius}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We are given the circumference of the circle as 12π units. Therefore, we can write:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 12\pi = 2\pi \times \text{radius}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To find the radius, we need to divide both sides of the equation by \(2\pi\):
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \text{radius} = \frac{12\pi}{2\pi} = \frac{12}{2} = 6 \text{ units}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Now that we have the radius, we can find the area of the square using the formula:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \text{Area} = \text{side}^2 = \text{radius}^2
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \text{Area} = 6^2 = 36 \text{ square units}
