[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Find $\left|\left(1+i\right)^6\right|$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m ###### One way to solve this problem is to use De Moivre's theorem. De Moivre's theorem states that for a complex number $z=a+bi$, the $n$-th power of $z$ can be expressed as $z^n=a^n+b^n+i(a^n-b^n)i$ .
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m In this problem, $z=1+i$ and $n=6$ , so we can start by computing the powers of $z$:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $z^1=z=1+i$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $z^2=(1+i)^2=1+2i-2=-1+2i$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $z^3=(1+i)^3=(1+i)(-1+2i)=-1+2i-1-2i-2i^2=-1+2i+1+2i+2=4i$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $z^4=z^3e^{-i\pi}=4ie^{-i\pi}$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $z^5=z^4e^{-i2\pi}=16ie^{-2\pi}$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $z^6=z^5e^{-i3\pi}=64e^{-3\pi}$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Now, we can see that $z^6=64e^{-3\pi}$, which is a real number. So, $\left|z^6\right|=\left|64e^{-3\pi}\right|=64e^{-\left(3\pi-2k\pi\right)}=64=8^2=8$ .
