[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m How many units apart is any pair of parallel sides of a regular hexagon with side of 6 units? Express your answer in simplest radical form.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To determine the distance between the parallel sides of a regular hexagon when each has a side length of 6 units, we first calculate the length of one side:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m # length of one side = 6 units 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m A regular hexagon with side length \( S \) can be divided into six equilateral triangles. The side length \( S \) of the hexagon's face forms one of these triangles. For a regular hexagon, the distance between any two vertices closer to the center is equal to one-third of the side length. This is because of the symmetry of the hexagon.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Thus, the distance between any two vertices, which are two vertices away from the center, is:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m # distance between vertices = \(\frac{S}{3}\)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Given that \( S = 6 \):
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ \text{distance between vertices} = \frac{6}{3} = 2 \text{ units} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Therefore, the distance between any two parallel sides of the hexagon is (2 units) expressed in simplest radical form. 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \boxed{2}<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 6\sqrt{3}
