[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To find the ones digit of $22^{22(11^{11})}$, we need to determine the last digit of the power $22^{22(11^{11})}$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We can use grouping theory to find the last digit of the power. Since the base 22 is congruent to 2 modulo 10, and $11^{11}$ is indeed a multiple of 11, the last digit of $11^{11}$ is 1. Multiplying it with 2 may not be divisible by 10, but we can proceed according to a simpler method.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We know that $11^{11}$ is congruent to 1 modulo 4, since 11/4 = 2.5, which means $11^{11}$ has the same remainder as 1 and 11 taken mod 4. So, $11^{11}$ is congruent to 1 modulo 4 and thus has the same last digit as $1^{11}$, which is the last digit of 1. Moreover, multiplying it by 2 also yields the last digit of 2. Hence, the last digit of $22(11^{11})$ will be the last digit of $22 \times 1$, which is $2$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Therefore, the ones digit of $22^{22(11^{11})}$ is $\boxed{2}$.emouthdi
