[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Hello!
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We are given the sides of triangle \(ABC\) that are lengths \(AB = 9\), \(BC = 10\), and \(AC = 11\), and two known measurements, \(AD = 4\) and \(AE = 7\). We aim to find the area of triangle \(ADE\).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m First, we use Apollonius's theorem for tangents of any angle, which states that in any triangle, the sum of the squares of any two sides is equal to the product of the lengths of the remaining two sides. Rearranged, these equations translate to:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m AB^2 + AC^2 = AD^2 + AE^2
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m BC^2 + AC^2 = AD^2 + AE^2
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m AB^2 + BC^2 = AD^2 + AE^2
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Given \(AB = 9\), \(BC = 10\), and \(AC = 11\), we perform these calculations of squares:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 1. \( ABC = 11^2 + 9^2 = 121 + 81 = 202\)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 2. \( AD = 4 \)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 3. \( AE = 7 \)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
