[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m A band has 72 members who will all be marching during halftime. They need to march in rows with the same number of students per row. If there must be between 5 and 20 students per row, in how many possible row-lengths can the band be arranged?
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To solve this problem, I need to find the possible row-lengths of the band that satisfies the condition of having between 5 and 20 students per row.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Let's start by finding the possible number of rows. If we want to have a row of n students, we need to find the possible number of students (n) that are both a multiple of n and any number between 5 and 20.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since 5 is one of the conditions, it means n has to be a multiple of 0. n=5, 10, 15, and 20 (in increments of 5) will satisfy the condition.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m If we want to avoid multiples of 5, we can include a constraint to exclude multiples of 5. If we keep the condition of all multiples of n, we only have 6 valid row-lengths. 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Starting from 6, if we keep adding 6, we will always have 10 students per row (still valid since each row should have 5 + 6 =11 students). We cannot make any more valid row-lengths other than 6.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m This means there are only 6 possible row-lengths.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
