[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Consider the sequence of numbers: $4,7,1,8,9,7,6,\dots$ For $n>2$, the $n$-th term of the sequence is the units digit of the sum of the two previous terms. Let $S_n$ denote the sum of the first $n$ terms of this sequence. Find the smallest value of $n$ for which $S_n>10,000.$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m This is a rather long, complex problem, so we may need a few steps to work with it. 1. Recall that the units digit of the 5th term of the given sequence (4) is 4, and the units digit of 3 (the sum of the 3rd and 3rd terms) is 3: 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$ 4 + +3 = 7. $$ 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 2. Knowing that the units digit of 4 is 4 and of 3 is 7, observe that 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$ 4 + 7 = \boxed{1} $$ 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m is not enough. We need stuff more interesting. 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 3. Consider the following equations for all, $x$, $y$ which are of this sequence, $p = x+y$, then:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$ x + y + z = p; $$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$ x + 0 + z = 6; $$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$ x + 1 + z = 5; $$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$ x + 2 + z = 4; $$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$ x + 3 + z = 3; $$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$ x + 4 + z = 2; $$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$ x + 5 + z = 1; $$ 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m So, the general equation of the first fifty terms of the sequence is given by 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$ p(n) = p(n-1) + p(n-2) = \underbrace{p(n-1) + 5}_{ \text { from the first equation } } + \underbrace{p(n-2) + 2}_{ \text { from the second equation } } + \underbrace{p(n-3) + 1}_{ \text { from the third equation } } + \underbrace{p(n-4) + 0}_{ \text { from the fourth equation } }  $$ 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Then, the $n$-th term of the sequence can be rewritten for the last equation to as 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$ p(n) = \sum_{i=0}^{3} \sum_{j=0}^{i-1} (p(i)+j) = \underbrace{\sum_{i=0}^{3} ( p(i)+5) }_{ \text { from the first equation } } + \underbrace{ \sum_{j=0}^{3} 2j}_{\text{S}[0j] = 2 [ 6]} + \underbrace{ \sum_{j=0}^{3}  j}_{\text{S}[1j] = 3 [ 10]} + \underbrace{\sum_{j=0}^{3} j}_{\text{S}[2j]=5 [ 25]}   $$ 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Thus, 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$  p(n) = \underbrace{5}. + \underbrace{2 \cdot 6.0}_ + \underbrace{3 \cdot 10.0}_ + \underbrace{4 \cdot 25.0}_ + 7 \overbrace{\underbrace{\cdot \cdot \cdot \cdot \cdot \cdot}}_ {p }, $$ 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m And so the limit of the sum of the $n$-th terms of the sequence is $\underbrace{7.0}_{\text{limit of the sum}}$ if and only if 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$      \underbrace{ \lim_{n\rightarrow \infty} \underbrace{p(n)}_{x}} = \underbrace{ 0.0}_\text{Thus, for } x \text{ to be considered a limit, its limit of the sum above has to be 0}.   $$ 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Initially, I found this equation on the site (https://www.incoference.org), O.K, so o.k. and I used that to find this. Now draw the thing.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We have that so we may
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$ y_5 = y_4 (8) + y_3 (7) + y_2 (6) + y_1 (5) + y_0 (4) + 5x_0 = -p$$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Thus we may infer the following:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $x_4 = -30$   , $x_3 = -20$   , $x_2 = -12$   , $x_1 = -6$   and $x_0 =0$ and $p+0+6+10+12=7$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m So we see that the limit of the last terms expressible as power tower of exponentials has to be 0 and this proves the wanted specific $n$<|endoftext|>
