[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m If 7 knicks = 2 knacks and 3 knacks = 4 knocks, how many knicks are equal to 24 knocks?
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We can start by understanding the relationships between the units of measurement and deriving the original formula.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Ticks difference between knicks and knacks = 1
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Ticks difference between knacks and knocks = 2
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Ticks difference between knocks and knicks = 3
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m It'll take 10 knocks to equal 1 tick.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We know, 7 knicks = 2 knacks, so 3 knacks = 4 knocks.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We can say it becomes tricky to know the difference of knacks and knocks, but we can say it there's 1 repeater for every 2 knacks. Also, we can say 3 knacks = 4 knocks.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Now, if 7 knicks are equal to 2 knacks, then 1 knick = 2/7 knacks.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m And, 3 knacks represent 4 knocks. So, the number of knocks in 7 knicks is 3/2 knicks.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Finally, 24 knocks divided by 7 knicks gives us 3-ticks.
