[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Four people can paint a house in six hours. How many hours would it take three people to paint the same house, assuming everyone works at the same rate?
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Let $x$ be the time taken for three people to paint the same house.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since they can paint the same part of the house in six hours, then the part they need to paint will be $\frac {1}{x}$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The total number of people is $4+3=\boxed{7}$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Therefore, $\frac {1}{x}=\frac {1}{7}\times 6= \frac {6}{7}$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Hence, the time taken for the three people to paint the house is $\boxed{\frac{7}{6}}$.
