[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Using the Factor Theorem, we see that $(x-5+\sqrt{2})$ and $(x-5-\sqrt{2})$ are factors, so we expect that their product will have this form. This leads us to the fact that: \[\left[(x^3 + bx + c)\right]\left[ (x^2 - 10x + 26)\right]\]\[\supset [0, 20] \subseteq \{1, 4, 7, 12, 15, 19\}\] The only possibilities to be integer roots on this range are $-3$ and $2$. We then have \[\left[x^3 + bx + c \right]\text{[}\{2\}|[0\}|-|-3=2\right]\\ \left[x - 2\right]\text{[}\{1\}|-|-2=2\right]\\ \left[x - 1\right]\text{[}\{3\}|-|-3=2\right] \\ \left[x - 1\right]\text{[}\{7\}|-|-7=2\right]\] The coefficients of $[x^3 + bx + c]$ can then be written as $6$, $4 + b$, and $3 + 2b$. Solving for $b$, we find that $b = -2$, and $c = 4$. The remaining root must be an integer. There are two possible integer roots that still promote the third equation. The possible values for $b$ yield $10$, $-2$, and/or $11$, while $c = 4$ yields $9$ and $12$. Any value would work, but because \[\left[x - 2\right]\text{[}\{1\}|-|-2=2\right]\\ \left[x - 1\right]\text{[}\{3\}|-|-3=2\right]\] The possible integer roots must then yield amounts of consecutive squares, resulting in the following quadratic factors: \[\left[x^3 + bx + c \right]\text{[}\{2\}|[0\}|-|-3=2\right]\\ \left[x^3 - 8x^2 + 44x + 33\right]\text{[},[x^3 + bx + c],\[0\}\]
