[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Let $n$ be a positive integer and $a,b$ be invertible integers modulo $n$ such that $a\equiv b^{-1}\pmod n$. What is the remainder when $ab$ is divided by $n$?
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m One can devise a method to solve the problem that is based on*.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Imagine the prime factorizations of $a$ and $b$ as follows:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m a = f_1^{e_1} f_2^{e_2} \cdots f_n^{e_n}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m b = g_1^{f_1} g_2^{f_2} \cdots g_n^{f_n}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m where $f_1, \cdots, f_n, g_1, \cdots, g_n$ are positive integers and $f_1, \cdots, f_n$ are pairwise distinct.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We will calculate the product
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m ab = (f_1^{e_1} f_2^{e_2} \cdots f_n^{e_n}) (g_1^{f_1} g_2^{f_2} \cdots g_n^{f_n}) = f_1^e f_2^e \cdots f_n^e g_1^{f_1} g_2^{f_2} \cdots g_n^{f_n}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m which, as soon as we consider $e$ and $f_i$ to be prime, can be splitted into two factored-exponentiation expressions:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m ab = (f_1^{e_1} f_2^{e_2}) (g_1^{f_1} g_2^{f_2}) \cdots (g_n^{f_n})
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since $\binom{n}{r}\equiv \begin{cases} 0 & r \nmid n \\ \binom{n}{r} & r=n \\ \binom{n}{r-1} & r> n \\ \end{cases}$ by the Chinese Remainder Theorem,
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m you can calculate $ab\equiv 1\pmod n$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Note that the Chinese Remainder Theorem is very powerful, but it only works in `special cases'.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The fact that $f_1, \cdots, f_n$ and $g_1, \cdots, g_n$ are multinomial is perhaps an intermediate $special$ case and might not be needed.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Nevertheless, if you allow yourself more flexibility, then the proof that the necessary reference equivalence relation is a congruence class can indeed take a nice look, proving a direct and algebraical proof still.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m What you want is
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m a\binom{n}{1} a \binom{n}{n-1} \cdots a \binom{n}{n-(r-1)} \equiv (ab)^2
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m ab\equiv \frac{(f_1^{e_1} f_2^{e_2})^2}{f_1^{n-1} f_2^{n-1}} \binom{n}{e_1+e_2-(r-1)} \pmod n
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \equiv \frac{(g_1^{f_1} g_2^{f_2})^2}{g_1^{f_1} g_2^{f_2}} \binom{n}{f_1+f_2-(r-1)} \pmod n
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \equiv (ab)^2 \pmod n
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m where $(ab)^2 \equiv 1 \pmod n$ addresses the trivial case.<|endoftext|>
