[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The expression $\dfrac{\sqrt{10}}{\sqrt[4]{10}}$ equals 10 raised to what power?
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To determine the power in which 10 is raised, we first need to reduce the given expression.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Angular acceleration is measured by how quickly the angular velocity changes:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \text{Angular acceleration} (\gamma) = \dfrac{\Delta \alpha}{\Delta t}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m For the given velocity equation, you can observe the angular acceleration. Angular acceleration is characterized by:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \alpha(t) &= (\tfrac{d\omega}{dt})(t)e^{\frac{KB}{\tau}} \\[5pt]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \right) &= (\tfrac{d\omega}{dt})(t)e^{\frac{(\alpha\pi)^2}{2M}} \\[5pt]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since $\gamma(t) = \alpha(0)$, we can denote $\alpha(0) = \alpha(V_1^{-} )$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \alpha(t) &= (\tfrac{d\omega}{dt})(t)e^{\frac{\alpha(V_1^{-})P}{\tau}} \\[5pt]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Amplitude is 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m A(t) &= \frac{\theta0}{\tau} \\[5pt] &= \frac{t0}{\tau} \\[5pt] &= t
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m And thus time change,
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m t &= t(t)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m So you can see that the angle of the radius will increase in ($CV = 3$) time units.<|endoftext|>
