[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m You can only modulate $2x^6$, and $-4x^4$ are both divisible by $x+3$, BUT you can't modulate $x$ directly. So, they both *may* be modulated by their respective "closest demons" (denote the demons as $k$ and $l$). 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m  
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m k = $x+3$, $l$ = $x$ (both distinct, but frequent enough for the 2 squares to meet)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m When Y = $l$ AND L = $-k$ OR Y = $k$ AND L = $-l$, the result is 0. This is the paradigm that module $x$ by itself satisfies: 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$Y \times X \equiv 0 \ iff Y \times X \equiv 0 mod 2x^6$$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m y $ \equiv 0 mod 1$ 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Now, these are squares, so we need the CRTs 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$\left\{\begin{array}{ll}  Y \equiv 0 mod 1\\ L \equiv 0 mod 2k\end{array}\right\} \lor
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \left\{\begin{array}{ll}  Y \equiv 0 mod 1\\ L \equiv 0 mod 3l\end{array}\right\}$$ 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m and the only solution to $Y \equiv 0 mod 1$ are $Y \equiv 0 mod 2k$ or $3k$ (which happens, because $2k$ has cycle length base 2 whereas 3 solutions have cycles of lenght 3 times the lowest common multiple). 
