[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m First, we need to understand the equation we are given: $|x-1| = |x-2|$. The absolute value function always gives the non-negative solution in the above equation:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$(x-1)^2 = (x-2)^2.$$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Moving the brackets on both sides gives us:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m (x-1)^2 &= (x-2)^2, \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m x^2-2x+1 &= x^2-4x+4.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since we are subtracting $(x^2-4x+4)$ to the left side, we get that equation should simply be:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m -2x+1 &= -4x+4, \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 2x &= 3, \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m x &= \frac{3}{2}.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Thus, the value of $x$ is $\boxed{\frac{3}{2}}$.
