[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Four people can paint a house in six hours. How many hours would it take three people to paint the same house, assuming everyone works at the same rate?
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To determine how long three people would take to paint the house, we start by understanding the painting rate. 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The rate of painters is given as 4 painters completing 50 square yards in 6 hours. First, let's calculate their individual rate:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \text{Rate} = \frac{\text{Complete Work}}{\text{Time in Hours}}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Given:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - 50 square yards
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - Time taken= 6 hours
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \text{Rate} = \frac{50 \, \text{square yards}}{6 \, \text{hours}}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \text{Rate} = \frac{25}{3} \, \text{square yards per hour}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Next, we need to find out how many square yards each person can paint in one hour (assuming they all work at the same speed):
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \text{People} = 3
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since we need to find out how many hours it will take for these three individuals to paint 50 square yards, we set up this proportion:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \frac{50 \, \text{square yards}}{6 \, \text{hours}} = \frac{x}{3 \, \text{hours}}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Solving for \( x \):
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m x = \frac{50 \times 3}{6}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m x = \frac{150}{6}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m x = 25 \, \text{square yards per hour}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Now, we calculate the time it would take one person to paint 25 square yards:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \text{Time} = \frac{25 \, \text{square yards}}{25 \, \text{square yards per hour}}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \text{Time} = 1 \, \text{hour}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Because everyone paints simultaneously, we repeat the process for three people:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \text{Time} = \frac{25 \, \text{square yards}}{25 \, \text{square yards per hour}} = 1 \, \text{hour}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Hence, it will take all three painters 1 hour to paint the house. Therefore, the answer is:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \boxed{1}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
