[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Find the projection of $\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}$ onto the plane $3x - y + 4z = 0.$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Step 1: Understand the concept of projection and geometric interpretation.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Projection is a transformation of a vector or a 3D point onto the plane it lies on. Geometrically, \(P(x,y,z)\) is a point on the plane \(3x - y + 4z = 0\), and \(\vec{P}\) is the projection of \(P\) onto the plane.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Step 2: Determine the direction of the projection.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since \(3x - y + 4z = 0\), the direction vector of the plane is \(\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -1 \\ 4 \end{pmatrix}\).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Step 3: Find the distance between the point on the plane and the origin.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The point \(\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}\) lies on the plane, so it satisfies the equation \(3x - y + 4z = 0\). Solve for the coefficients in terms of the variable:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \begin{pmatrix} 3x \\ 1 \\ 4x \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \begin{pmatrix} x \\ \left(4 - 1\right)y \\ 3 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m This gives us:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m x = 1, \quad y = 3 + \left(4 - 1\right) = 6, \quad z = 3
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m So the coordinates of the point on the plane are \(\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}\).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Step 4: Calculate the distance between \(\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}\) and the origin \(\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}\).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Using the distance formula:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \text{Distance} = \sqrt{1^2 + 2^2 + 6^2} = \sqrt{1 + 4 + 36} = \sqrt{41}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Thus, the projection of \(\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 3 \end{pmatrix}\) onto the plane \(3x - y + 4z = 0\) is:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \boxed{\sqrt{41}}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
