[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Find the matrix $\mathbf{P}$ such that for any vector $\mathbf{v},$ $\mathbf{P} \mathbf{v}$ is the projection of $\mathbf{v}$ onto the $y$-axis.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Matrix $\mathbf{P}$ will be a $2\times 1$ matrix, where the first column ($1\times 1$) is $\begin{pmatrix} {u_{2\mathrm{,x}} } \\ {u_{3\mathrm{,x}} } \end{pmatrix}$, and the second column ($1\times 1$) is $\begin{pmatrix} {u_{2\mathrm{,y}} } \\ {u_{3\mathrm{,y}} } \end{pmatrix}$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Then, the projection of $\mathbf{v}$ onto the $y$-axis is given by the first part of the matrix, that is:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$\begin{pmatrix} {u_{2\mathrm{,x}}, \\u_{2\mathrm{,y}\mathrm{-axis}}} \end{pmatrix} \,\cdot\,\, \begin{pmatrix} { {v_{x\mathrm{-axis}}} } \\ {u_{3\mathrm{,y}\mathrm{-axis}} } \end{pmatrix}$$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Where $(\mathbf{P}\mathbf{v})_{2\mathrm{,y\mathrm{-axis}}}=v_{2\mathrm{-axis}}$, so we can simplify the above integral to:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$P_\mathrm{y\mathrm{-axis}}\, \mathbf{v}_{2\mathrm{-axis}} = \begin{pmatrix} {0} \\ \, u _{2\mathrm{,x} } \end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix} {0} \\ \, u _{2\mathrm{,y} } \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} {0} \\ \, u _{2\mathrm{,x} } + u _{2\mathrm{,y} } \end{pmatrix}$$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Therefore, the matrix will be:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$\mathbf{P} =\begin{pmatrix} {1} & {0} \\ {0} & {1} \end{pmatrix}$$
