[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m If $x$ and $y$ are positive real numbers such that $5x^2 + 10xy = x^3 + 2x^2 y,$ what is the value of $x$?
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m If you multiplied both sides by $x$ on the left side and rearranged, you would have
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m (5x^2 + 10xy) - (x^3 + 2x^2 y) = 0
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since you're dealing with $f(x) = 5x^2 + 10xy - x^3 - 2x^2y$, we can calculate mathematical properties (which you might not fully understand) for $f(x)$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Let's factor $f(x)$:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m &5x^2 + 10xy - x^3 - 2x^2y \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m &= x(5x + 10y - x^2y - 2xy) \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m &= x(5x^2 + 10xy - x^3 - 2x^2y - xy) \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m &= x(5x^2 + 10xy - x^3 + 3x^2y - xy) \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m &= x(4x^2 + y + xy)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m So, $x$ multiplication along the lhs means that the negative third terms in the middle are cancelled out, while $x^2$, $x$, and $y$ do not change especially when you allow negative $x$ as it will still cancel out the $-(x^3+x^2 y)$ term on the rhs.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Your third term on the left hand side seems to be a sign error.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Rewrite:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m &5x^2 + 10xy - x^3 - 2x^2y \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m &= 5(\frac{3x}{2})^2 - 2x(\frac{3x}{2})^2y - x^2 - (\frac{3x}{2})^2y \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m &= 5 \frac{27x^2}{4} + (\frac{27x^3}{4}-2 \frac{3x^2y}{2}y) - x^2 - (\frac{27x^2y}{4}) \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m &= \frac{67.5x^2}{4} - x^2 - \frac{3x^2y}{2}y - (\frac{27x^2y}{4}) \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m &= (27x^2 - \frac{27x^4}{4}) - ((\frac{3x^2y}{2}y - 1)(x^2 - \frac{27x^2}{4})) \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m &= 27x^2 - \frac{27x^4}{4} - \frac{3x^4y^2/y -27x^2y^2/y}{2}y(y - \frac{27x^2}{4}) \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m &= 27x^2 - \frac{27x^4}{4} - \frac{3x^4y^4/y -27x^2y^4/y}{2}y(y - \frac{27x^2}{4}) \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m &= 27x^2 - \frac{27x^4}{4} - \frac{27x^2y^4}{20y}y(y - \frac{27x^2}{4}) \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m &= 27x^2 - \frac{27x^4}{4} - \frac{27x^4y^4}{20}y(y - \frac{27x^2}{4})\\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m &= - (27x^2 + \frac{27x^4y^4}{20}y(y - \frac{27x^2}{4}))
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since we clearly see the equal signs rearranged, this means $x = 1$.<|endoftext|>
