[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The planet Xavier follows an elliptical orbit with its sun at one focus.  At its nearest point (perigee), it is 2 astronomical units (AU) from the sun, while at its furthest point (apogee) it is 12 AU away.  When Xavier is midway along its orbit, as shown, how far is it from the sun, in AU?
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m [asy]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m unitsize(1 cm);
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m path ell = xscale(2)*arc((0,0),1,-85,265);
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m filldraw(Circle((0,-1),0.1));
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m filldraw(Circle((-1.4,0),0.2),yellow);
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m draw(ell,Arrow(6));
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m [/asy]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To solve the problem, we start by understanding the given information. Xavier is a planet following an elliptical orbit around its sun. We know the following:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 1. Perigee (\(r_p\)): This is the closest distance of Xavier from the sun.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    r_p = 2 \text{ AU}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 2. Apogee (\(r_a\)): This is the farthest distance of Xavier from the sun.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    r_a = 12 \text{ AU}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 3. The planet is at a midway point when Xavier is near perigee, meaning \(r_p\) is halved compared to the apogee (\(r_a\)).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    r_a = 2 \times \frac{r_p}{2}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    Since \(r_p = 2 \text{ AU}\), we substitute to find \(r_a\):
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    r_a = 2 \times \frac{2 \text{ AU}}{2} = 2 \text{ AU}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 4. We need to find the distance of Xavier from the sun when it is at midway point.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since the planet is at the midway point, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m r_p = r_{\text{mid}} \quad \text{and} \quad r_a = r_{\text{mid}}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We know that:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m r_{\text{mid}} = \frac{r_a}{2} = \frac{2 \text{ AU}}{2} = 1 \text{ AU}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Hence, Xavier is at 1 AU from the sun when it is midway along its orbit.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Conclusion: The distance of Xavier from the sun when it is midway along its orbit is:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \boxed{1}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
