[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We are given that $x + y + z = 1.$  To find the maximum value of $x + y^2 + z^3,$ we need to maximize the function of the variables $x,$ $y^2,$ and $z^3$ subject to the constraint $x + y + z = 1.$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We minimize this function by setting all of the variables $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ to 0. In that case, we get $x + y^2 + z^3 = 0 + 0 + 0 = 0.$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m However, since $x + y + z = 1,$ we know that $x \leq 1,$ $y \leq 1,$ and $z \leq 1.$  We can choose $x = z = 1,$ which gives us $1 + 1^2 + 1^3 = 3.$  This is the maximum value of the function when all three variables are minimized.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Therefore, the maximum value of $x + y^2 + z^3$ is $\boxed{3}.$<|endoftext|>
