[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m First, let's find the length of one side of each smaller square. The area of one square is given by the square of its side length. We have six squares, so the total area is \(6 \times \text{side length}^2 = 294\). Let \(s\) be the side length of one square. Then,
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[6s^2 = 294 \implies s^2 = \frac{294}{6} = 49 \implies s = \sqrt{49} = 7\]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Now, let's find the total length of all six sides. These lengths are \(s\), \(s\), \(s\), \(s\), \(s\) (truncated to two decimal places), and so on. The total length can be calculated as:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[6s + 10 + 10 + 10 + \text{(...)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m ...where the dots denote truncation of the series). This simplifies to:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[6 \times 7 + \text{(...)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m ...}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since 10 appears twice, we treat the sum as \(10 \times 2 = 20\).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The perimeter of the region is the sum of all sides:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[6s + 10 + 10 + 10 + \cdots = 6s + 10 \times 3 = 6 \times 7 + 30 = 42 + 30 = 72\]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m So, the perimeter of the region is \(\boxed{72}\) centimeters.<|endoftext|>
