[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m What is the least common multiple of 6, 8, and 10?
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Step 1: Identify the numbers we're dealing with. In this case, we have 6, 8, and 10.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Step 2: Find the least common multiple (LCM) of the three numbers. The LCM is the smallest number that is a multiple of each of the given numbers.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Step 3: Find the common factors of the three numbers. The common factors are the numbers that divide all of them without a remainder.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Step 4: Identify the greatest prime factor (GPPF) of each common factor. This is the prime number that appears the most in all the common factors.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Step 5: Multiply the GPPF of each common factor to get the LCM. Subtract the remainder from the highest GPPF of a common factor to obtain the GPPF of the smallest fraction.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Step 6: The LCM step-by-step calculation:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - First common factor: GPPF1 = 1
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - Next \( 8 = 1 \times 8\)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - Next \( 10 = 1 \times 10 \) (GPPF2 = 10)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - Subdivide \( 10 \) by \( 1 \) until there is no left, which gives \( 10\) as \( (1 \times 10) \div 10 = (1 \times 10 \div 10) \)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - Step 4: The GPPF of the smallest fraction is 10.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - Least common multiple: \( \text{LCM} = \text{GPPF}_1 \times \text{GPPF}_2 = 1 \times 10 = 10 \)
