[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Find the number of real roots of
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[2x^{2001} + 3x^{2000} + 2x^{1999} + 3x^{1998} + \dots + 2x + 3 = 0.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To determine the number of real roots of the polynomial equation:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[2x^{2001} + 3x^{2000} + 2x^{1999} + 3x^{1998} + \dots + 2x + 3 = 0,\]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m we will analyze the behavior of the polynomial by considering it modulo more general integers.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m First, consider the polynomial modulo 2:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[x^{2001} \equiv x^1 \equiv x \pmod{2}.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Similarly,
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[x^{2000} \equiv x^1 \equiv x \pmod{2}.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[x^{1999} \equiv x^1 \equiv x \pmod{2}.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[x^{1998} \equiv x^1 \equiv x \pmod{2}.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m and so forth, for any integer \( k \geq 1 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Thus, the polynomial \( x^{2001} + 3x^{2000} + \cdots + 3x + 3 \) modulo 2 will be
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ x + 3 \equiv x + 3 \pmod{2}.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Therefore,
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ \text{the sum of the coefficients is } x + 3. \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Now consider the polynomial modulo 2 individually with each of the terms:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 2x = 2x &\equiv 0 \pmod{2} \text{ if } x = 0, \text{ or } y \neq 0. \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 3x = 3x &\equiv 0 \pmod{2} \text{ if } x = 0, \text{ or } y \neq 0. \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 2x^{1999} = 2x \cdot x^{1998} \equiv 0 \pmod{2} \text{ if } x = 0 \text{ or } y \neq 0.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Thus, the polynomial modulo 2 is for all \( y \neq 0 \):
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ 0 + 3y \equiv 3y \pmod{2}.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The only solution to \( y \equiv 0 \pmod{2} \) is \( y = 0 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Thus, the polynomial \( 0 + 3y \equiv 3y \pmod{2} \) cannot help us determine the number of real roots. Since none of the terms \( x, 3x, 2x^{1999}, \ldots, 2x \) gives positive odd numbers modulo 2, the polynomial has neither positive nor negative odd numbers modulo 2, and therefore it cannot have any real roots.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m  Conclude that the scheme does not describe any real roots.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
