[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Hey there, little buddy! To figure out $\sin 90^\circ$, let's think about what values come with this angle. We know that $90^\circ$ is one of the special angles that cosine and sine can't handle very easily, so let's see which values we can find:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 1. Imagine placing a lighted bulb centrally on a dark desk. If you look straight at the lighted bulb and the desk, all those angles involved with the mirror (what we call diagonals) will be looked into with the light turned off. That means, we can say that $\sin$ of any such angle will be either $0$ or $1$ because those arc direction changes $\sin a = \sin (180^\circ + a) = \sin 180^\circ$. So, $\sin 90^\circ = 1$ because $90^\circ$ has a special value of $1$ for sine.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 2. Returning to our former mock scenario, our $90^\circ$ lights are those that serve as mirrors. If we could see just two directions and still finance our lighted bulbs conveniently with just one strike for a straight path, we know that the sine angle $90^\circ$, like the straight path angle, turns out to be $\sin 90^\circ = 1$, since turning a light cone makes $90^\circ$ and $90^\circ + 180^\circ$ share the space exactly — both form a ninety-degree quarter circle when combined.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Therefore, $\sin 90^\circ = 1$, your answer!
