[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m A blue plane, a red plane and a white plane are waiting to take off from an airport that has two runways. Planes must take off from the airport one at a time, but can take off from either runway. In how many ways can the three takeoffs be scheduled? (One such way is the blue plane on runway A, followed by the red plane on runway B, followed by the white plane on runway B.)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Here's the walkthrough and final answer.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The problem is asking us to determine in how many ways the three planes can be scheduled, believing that order doesn't matter.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We can count the possibilities by calculating the number of permutations of the 3 items that being the 3 planes and then scheduling them into the 3 positions.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m In this case, we first choose 3 positions on the three runways, which can be done in \\(P^3_3\\) ways.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Then, we arrange the 3 planes in order, which can be done in \\(P^6_3\\) ways.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Therefore, the total number of scheduled ways is \(\boxed{360}\).<|endoftext|>
