[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m An ice cream shop offers 6 kinds of ice cream. What is the greatest number of two scoop sundaes that can be made such that each sundae contains two types of ice cream and no two sundaes are the same combination?
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To solve this problem, we need to first figure out all possible combinations of two scoops from the six available ice cream options (a @{$P=[1,1,1,1,1,1]}). These six combinations are: @{$P=[1,1,1,1,1,1]}, @{$P=[2,1,1,1,1,1]}, ..., @{$P=[2,2,1,1,1,1]}.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m There are exactly two unique sets of three scoops from these six combinations. These sets are: @{$P=[1,2,3]}` and ` @{$P=[1,4,10]}`.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m There are exactly one unique combination of two scoops of one type of ice cream. This set is: @{$P=[1,6]}`.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Let's consider the possibilities for the flavor in two scoops:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 1. $\mathsf{If \ color{green}{the yogurt and lemon topic}\ \ }, \mathsf{Then \ color{green}{the apple topping is \ color{red}{not} \ color{green}{any of the two scoops}}\ \ }$,
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $\mathsf{If \ color{green}{no yogurt and lemon topping}\ \ } \mathsf{Then \ color{red}{the apple topping is \ color{green}{any of the two scoops}}}$,
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $m=\{1,q,r,a,b,c,d,e\}, p=\{2,1,i,j,k,l\}$ with $2^m \subset p^m $, and $q,b,e \notin p$. $q,b,e \in p^m \Rightarrow q,b,e \notin p\ \ \color{green}{\Longrightarrow} 0$ states. $i \notin p \Rightarrow i \in q \cup r \cup a \cup b \cup c \cup d \cup e$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Hence the answer is $2$.
