[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m a. To simplify \(\sqrt{192}\), we first factor 192 into two factors that multiply together to give 192. The prime factorization of 192 is:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ 192 = 2 \times 12 \times 12 = 2 \times 2 \times 6 \times 12 = 2 \times 2 \times 2 \times 6 \times 2 \times 2 = 2^4 \times 3 \times 2 \times 2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m From this factorization, we identify that \(\sqrt{192}\) can be broken down into:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ \sqrt{192} = \sqrt{2^4 \times 3 \times 2 \times 2} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We can further factor out the perfect square from under the square root:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ \sqrt{192} = \sqrt{(2^2 \times 2)^2 \times 3} = \sqrt{(2^2)^2 \times 2^2 \times 3} = (2 \times 2 \times 2) \sqrt{3} = 8 \sqrt{3} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m So the simplified form of \(\sqrt{192}\) is:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ \boxed{8 \sqrt{3}} \]<|endoftext|>
