[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Steve has an isosceles triangle with base 8 inches and height 10 inches. He wants to cut it into eight pieces that have equal areas, as shown below. To the nearest hundredth of an inch what is the number of inches in the greatest perimeter among the eight pieces? [asy]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m size(150);
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m defaultpen(linewidth(0.7));
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m draw((0,0)--(8,0));
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m for(int i = 0; i < 9; ++i){
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m draw((4,10)--(i,0));
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m }
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m draw((0,-0.5)--(8,-0.5),Bars(5));
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m label("$8''$",(0,-0.5)--(8,-0.5),S);
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m [/asy]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To find the greatest perimeter among the eight pieces of the isosceles triangle, we need to consider how the pieces are cut from the original triangle and how they show equal areas when joined.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m First, we recognize that a square is interchangeable with an isosceles triangle of equal base and height. Since the square fits within the base of the isosceles triangle, the square's area will be equal to half the area of the triangle.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The base of the triangle is 8 inches and the height is 10 inches. Therefore, the area of the triangle is:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} \times \text{base} \times \text{height} = \frac{1}{2} \times 8 \times 10 = 40 \text{ square inches}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m When we cut the triangle into eight pieces, each piece will have half the size of the entire triangle. This means each piece will have an area of \(20\) square inches. These eight pieces will be joined to form seven geometric shapes:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 1. One equilateral triangle (with a base of 4 inches and equal sides).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 2. A square.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 3. Another isosceles triangle with a base of 4 inches and equal sides.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m ### Cutting the Triangle Permanently
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since the cutting is permanent and the square and isosceles triangle are cut side by side, the two isosceles triangles share a common base of 4 inches. We can append these two in a way that fits them back together effortlessly.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The perimeter of the complete equation is:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m (4+8)+10=22 \text{ inches}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Let's compute this more accurately using Python to confirm the calculation.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m ```python
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m # Given dimensions
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m base_triangle = 8
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m height_triangle = 10
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
