[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m First, let's find out how many chiselypulations (y) are equivalent to 1 chiliys (x). We know that:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 1 chiliys = 3 chiselypulations
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m So, we can write:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \( 100 \text{ chiliys} = 1 \times 3 \)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m This equation gives us the number of chillies equivalent to 1 chiselypulation.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Now, let's find out how many chiselypulations (y) are equivalent to 100 chiliys. Using the same ratio:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \( y = 100 \text{ chiliys} = 1 \times 3 \)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m This equation gives us the number of chillies equivalent to 100 chillies.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Now, we can solve for y by dividing y by 100 (since we are dealing with individual chillies):
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \( y = \frac{100 \text{ chillies}}{100 \text{ chillies}} \)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
