[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m What is the remainder when the product of the $5$ smallest prime numbers is divided by $42$?
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Let the five smallest prime numbers be P1, P2, P3, P4, and P5.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The product of these primes is P1 \* P2 \* P3 \* P4 \* P5.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We need to find the remainder when this product is divided by 42.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since 42 = 2 \* 31, we can further simplify this product modulo 2 and modulo 31.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m First, find the product modulo 2: P1 \* P2 \* P3 \* P4 \* P5.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m If at least one of P1, P2, P3, P4, or P5 is even after removing the factor of 2, then the product will be even. If at least one of the numbers is odd, the product will be odd. Both statements are independent, so the product of all five primes will always be odd.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since the remainder when dividing by 2 is always 1 (because every number is odd except for the prime 2), the remainder when dividing by 2 is 1.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Now, let's find the product modulo 31. We can reduce each of the eight prime numbers modulo 31 first, and then take their product modulo 31.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m P1 \* P2 \* P3 \* P4 \* P5 \* P6 \* P7 \* P8 \equiv P1 \mod 31
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m This is because each prime number modulo 31 is simply the original number modulo 31.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Next, P2 \* P3 \* P4 \* P5 \* P6 \* P7 \* P8 \equiv P2^2 \mod 31
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Finally, P3 \* P4 \* P5 \* P6 \* P7 \* P8 \equiv P3^2 \mod 31
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since the square of every prime number modulo 31 is either 1 or -1 (mod 31), it follows that P1^2 \* P2^2 \* P3^2 \* P4^2 \* P5^2 \* P6^2 \* P7^2 \* P8^2 \equiv (-1)^{129} \equiv 1 \mod 31
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Therefore, the remainder when dividing the product of the five smallest prime numbers modulo 31 is 1.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Now, find the remainder when the product modulo 2 and modulo 31 is put together. This is because we have two different moduli, and the product modulo 31 is already between 0 and 2 (since the remaining product has a remainder of 1). Thus, the question is redundant as it can be easily answered with just one moduli.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Finally, since the product modulo 2 and modulo 31 is the same (remember, every product modulo 2 has a remainder of 1, but modulo 31, every product has a remainder of 1), the remainder when divided by 42 is 1.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
