[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Consider sequences of positive real numbers of the form $x, 2000, y, \dots$ in which every term after the first is 1 less than the product of its two immediate neighbors. For how many different values of $x$ does the term 2001 appear somewhere in the sequence?
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The plain way to want to solve this equation:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - First limit the values of x that doesn't give any valid yields. 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - Then using the basic rule of non-negative integers have $x = 1 \le x \le 0.75$. 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - Using the standard rule of finite sum have $2001 = x + 2000 + 1 - (x-1) - (2000 - 1)$. The right hand side simplifies to $2004$. So only 2043 values satisfies the original equation. 
