[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Yes, that is a beautiful problem!
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 🤔Your goal is to find the least prime number greater than 25 that will have a remainder of 2 when divided by 25.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Let's denote the number we're looking for as \( p \). We want \( p \) to be a prime number greater than 25, and it should leave a remainder of 2 when divided by 25, i.e., \( p \equiv 2 \pmod{25} \).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To satisfy this condition, the number \( p \) must leave a remainder when divided by 25, and the greatest common divisor (gcd) of \( p \) and 25 should be 1. This is because 25 and any prime number greater than 25 share a factor (25 in this case).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m So, we are looking for the smallest prime number greater than 25 that satisfies \( p \equiv 2 \pmod{25} \). This prime number is 27 because:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ p = 27 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ r = 27 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ 27 \div 25 = 1 \text{ remainder } 2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Finally, we check the gcd of 27 and 25:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ \gcd(27, 25) = 1 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Thus, \( 27 \) is indeed the number you're looking for, as it is the least prime number greater than 25 that leaves a remainder of 2 when divided by 25. So, the answer is:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ \boxed{27} \]<|endoftext|>
