[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Given that $\angle PQR = 65^\circ$ and $\angle QRC = 30^\circ$, we can use the fact that the tangent to the circle at the point of tangency creates an inscribed triangle.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Because $PQ$ is tangent to the circle at point $Q$, $\angle PRQ = \angle IQR = 90^\circ - \angle QRC = 60^\circ$ ( because $PQ$ is tangent and thus perpendicular to the radius at the point of tangency).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $\angle PRQ = 60^\circ$, and $\angle IQR = 60^\circ$, so $\angle QPR = 60^\circ - 30^\circ = 30^\circ$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m So $\angle QPR = \boxed{30}$, which is congruent with $\angle QRC$, as the argument for congruence applies since they are homothetic because $C$ is the center of the circle and the radii are equal that meet at $C$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The answer is: 30<|endoftext|>
