[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The lengths of two sides of a triangle are 33 units and 42 units. The third side also has an integral length. What is the least possible number of units in the perimeter of the triangle?
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To find the least possible number of units in the perimeter of the triangle, we need to consider the triangle inequality theorem. The theorem states that the sum of the three sides must be greater than the length of any single side and equal to the difference of the lengths of any two sides.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Given the sides 33 units and 42 units, let the third side be represented as \( x \) units. The triangle inequality theorem gives us the following conditions for \( x \):
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 1. \( x + 33 > 42 \)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 2. \( x + 42 > 33 \)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 3. \( 33 + 42 > x \)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Simplifying these inequalities:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 1. \( x + 33 > 42 \)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    x > 42 - 33
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    x > 9
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 2. \( x + 42 > 33 \)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    x > 33 - 42
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    x > -9 \quad \text{(This is always true for an integer length greater than 9, so no need to worry about this condition)}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 3. \( 33 + 42 > x \)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    x < 75
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Combining these results, we need \( x \) to satisfy both \( x > 9 \) and \( x < 75 \). Thus, \( x \) can be any integer in the range from 10 to 74 inclusive.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The least value for \( x \) is 10. Therefore, the third side must be 10 units. Now we calculate the perimeter of the triangle:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \text{Perimeter} = 33 + 42 + 10 = 85
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Thus, the least possible number of units in the perimeter of the triangle is \(\boxed{85}\).<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
