[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Find the coefficient of $x^{49}$ in the expansion of
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[(x + 1)(x + 3)(x + 5) \dotsm (x + 95)(x + 97)(x + 99).\]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To find the coefficient of \(x^{49}\) in the expansion of \(\left( x + 1 \right) \left( x + 3 \right) \left( x + 5 \right) \dotsm \left( x + 95 \right) \left( x + 97 \right) \left( x + 99 \right)\), we need to consider how the constant term \(1\) acts as a "constant factor" in the product.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Each factor \((x + k)\) in the product has an \(x\) term but no constant term, and the product \(1 \cdot x \cdot x \cdot \ldots \cdot x = x^5\). This means the only way to obtain \(x^{49}\) is as the product \((x + 1)x + (x + 3)x + \ldots + (x + 99)\). This is because:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m (x + 1)x + (x + 3)x + \ldots + (x + 99) = 49x
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since the term \(49x\) has an \(x\) factor with the smallest exponent in an increasing sequence of non-negative integers whose exponents sum up to 49, the coefficient of \(x^{49}\) will be 49.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Therefore, the coefficient of \(x^{49}\) in the expansion is \(\boxed{49}\).<|endoftext|>
