[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Let's begin by finding \( f(1) \). According to the definition of the function \( f \):
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ f(x) =
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \begin{cases}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m x/2 & \quad \text{if } x \text{ is even}, \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 3x+1 & \quad \text{if } x \text{ is odd}.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \end{cases}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since 1 is an odd number, we use the second part of the definition:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ f(1) = 3 \cdot 1 + 1 = 3 + 1 = 4. \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Next, we find \( f(f(1)) = f(4) \). Again, 4 is an even number, so we use the first part of the definition:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ f(4) = \frac{4}{2} = 2. \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Then, we find \( f(f(f(1))) = f(2) \). Since 2 is an even number, we use the first part of the definition:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ f(2) = \frac{2}{2} = 1. \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Finally, we find \( f(f(f(f(1)))) = f(1) \). As previously determined, 1 is an odd number, so we use the second part of the definition:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ f(1) = 3 \cdot 1 + 1 = 3 + 1 = 4. \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Putting everything together, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ f(f(f(f(1)))) = f(1) = 4. \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Therefore, the final answer is:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ \boxed{4} \]<|endoftext|>
