[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Yes, I understand. 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The equation of a circle with center $\left(-6, 2\right)$ and radius $10 \text{ units}$ has the standard form:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m # (x - \displaystyle -6)^2 + (y - \displaystyle -2)^2 = \displaystyle 10^2 #. 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Given that the circle is on the $y$-axis, the point with $x$-coordinate $0$ is on the circle. This point needs to satisfy the circle's equation. Substituting $x$-coordinate with $0$, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m # 0 - 6)^2 + (y - \displaystyle -2)^2 = 10^2 # or 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m # (0 - 10)^2 + (y - \displaystyle -2)^2 = 10^2 # or 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m # (-10)^2 + (y - \displaystyle -2)^2 = 10^2 #.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Subtract $(y - \displaystyle -2)^2$ from both sides of the equation, simplifying to:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m # (-10)^2 = 10^2 #.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m It can be solved quickly symbolically to get $y = \displaystyle -7$ (ageing on $-7$ will satisfy the hidden symbol!), and we can now play nicely with addition.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Hence, the sum of the $y$-coordinates of such points on circle $B$ that is also on $y$-axis will be:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m # -7 \displaystyle + 10 = \boxed{3}#.<|endoftext|>
