[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To solve for \(n\) in the equation \(11 \equiv n \pmod{9}\), we need to find an integer \(n\) such that when 11 is divided by 9, the remainder is \(n\). This can be expressed mathematically as:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[11 \equiv n \pmod{9}\]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m This means that there exists an integer \(k\) such that:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[11 = 9k + n\]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m By isolating \(n\), we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[n = 11 - 9k\]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since \(11 \equiv n \pmod{9}\) implies that \(n\) is an integer, the value of \(n\) must be a value that keeps \(11\) within a certain range when divided by 9. We can determine this by checking which values of \(n\) between 0 and 8 satisfy the equation.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Let's check each value:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - For \(n = 0\), \(11 \div 9 = 1\) remainder 2, so \(11 \not\equiv 0 \pmod{9}\)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - For \(n = 1\), \(11 \div 9 = 1\) remainder 2, so \(11 \not\equiv 1 \pmod{9}\)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - For \(n = 2\), \(11 \div 9 = 1\) remainder 2, so \(11 \not\equiv 2 \pmod{9}\)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - For \(n = 3\), \(11 \div 9 = 1\) remainder 2, so \(11 \not\equiv 3 \pmod{9}\)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - For \(n = 4\), \(11 \div 9 = 1\) remainder 2, so \(11 \not\equiv 4 \pmod{9}\)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - For \(n = 5\), \(11 \div 9 = 1\) remainder 2, so \(11 \not\equiv 5 \pmod{9}\)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - For \(n = 6\), \(11 \div 9 = 1\) remainder 2, so \(11 \not\equiv 6 \pmod{9}\)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - For \(n = 7\), \(11 \div 9 = 1\) remainder 2, so \(11 \not\equiv 7 \pmod{9}\)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - For \(n = 8\), \(11 \div 9 = 1\) remainder 2, so \(11 \equiv 8 \pmod{9}\)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Therefore, the only value of \(n\) that satisfies the equation \(11 \equiv n \pmod{9}\) is \(8\).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The answer is \(\boxed{8}\).<|endoftext|>
