[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Given $\xi \sim B(10, 0.6)$, find the value of $P(\xi < 6)$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To find the probability \( P(\xi < 6) \) for a binomial random variable \(\xi\) with \( n = 10 \) and \( p = 0.6 \), we can use the cumulative distribution function (CDF) of the binomial distribution. The probability mass function of a binomial random variable is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ P(\xi = k) = \binom{n}{k} p^k (1-p)^{n-k} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m where \(\binom{n}{k}\) is the binomial coefficient. For \( n = 10 \) and \( p = 0.6 \), we need to find \( P(\xi < 6) = P(\xi = 0) + P(\xi = 1) + P(\xi = 2) + P(\xi = 3) + P(\xi = 4) + P(\xi = 5) + P(\xi = 6) \).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m First, we calculate \( P(\xi = 0) \):
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ P(\xi = 0) = \binom{10}{0} (0.6)^0 (0.4)^{10} = 1 \cdot 1 \cdot 0.4^{10} = 0.4^{10} = 0.0001048576 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Next, we calculate \( P(\xi = 1) \):
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ P(\xi = 1) = \binom{10}{1} (0.6)^1 (0.4)^9 = 10 \cdot 0.6 \cdot 0.4^9 = 10 \cdot 0.6 \cdot 0.000262144 = 0.000157352 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m For \( P(\xi = 2) \):
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ P(\xi = 2) = \binom{10}{2} (0.6)^2 (0.4)^8 = \frac{10 \cdot 9}{2} \cdot (0.6)^2 \cdot (0.4)^8 = 45 \cdot 0.36 \cdot 0.004096 = 0.06526256 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m For \( P(\xi = 3) \):
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ P(\xi = 3) = \binom{10}{3} (0.6)^3 (0.4)^7 = \frac{10 \cdot 9 \cdot 8}{6} \cdot (0.6)^3 \cdot (0.4)^7 = 120 \cdot 0.216 \cdot 0.00027376 \approx 0.009073872 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since \( \xi \) is a binomial random variable, the value of \( P(\xi = 4) \) is \( 0 \), and the value of \( P(\xi = 5) \) is also \( 0 \). Therefore:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ P(\xi < 6) = P(\xi = 0) + P(\xi = 1) + P(\xi = 2) + P(\xi = 3) + P(\xi = 4) + P(\xi = 5) \approx 0.0001048576 + 0.000157352 + 0.000157352 + 0.000157352 + 0 + 0 = 0.0012225 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Rounding to six decimal places, the probability \( P(\xi < 6) \) is:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ P(\xi < 6) \approx 0.0012225 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m So the final answer is:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ \boxed{0.0012225} \]
