[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To find the smallest three-digit multiple of 13, we need to identify the smallest three-digit number that is also divisible by 13.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The smallest three-digit number is 100.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To check if 100 is divisible by 13, we can divide it by 13.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[\dfrac{100}{13} \approx 7.6923\]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since 100 is not a whole number, we round up 7 to the next higher whole number and divide again.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[\dfrac{100+8}{13} = \dfrac{108}{13} \approx 7.7692\]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Again, 7.7692 rounds up to 8.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m So, the smallest three-digit multiple of 13 is 108.
