[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Given: $x+4609 \equiv 2104 \pmod{12}$, and we want to find the smallest positive integer that satisfies the congruence, i.e., we want the least positive integer $x$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Step 1:  First, we observe that the left side of the congruence represents the sum of digits of $1304609$. And the right side represents the sum of digits of $2104$, i.e., $1+3+0+4+6+0+9 = 21$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Step 2:  Notice that $2104$ is an even number, so its sum of digits is also even. Since we are only interested in even numbers here, we can ignore the sign.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Step 3:  Therefore, the greatest common divisor (another helpful way to interpret to symbols used here) of both $1304+609$ and $2104$, will facilitate us in solving the problem. Since $609 = 3 \cdot 203 = 3 \cdot 7 \cdot 29 = 3 \cdot 7 \cdot 11 + 31$ and $203 = 13 \cdot 17 = 13 \cdot 11 + 12$, the greatest common divisor will be $2$. Thus, 'even numbers' will be the $x_{\text {even }}$, which will be cancelled out in the above-negative congruence.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Step 4:  Instead, notice that the numbers $1304+609=1917$, because wherever the product of prime factors $2$, $3$, and $7$ multiply $13$, the product becomes $1917$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Step 5:  We can write: $2104 \equiv 1304-1917+2104 \pmod{31}$,
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Step 6:  because $1304 + 609 = 2104-\textcolor{red}{1917} + 2104 \equiv 0 \pmod{31}$, and $2 \text{ (: 2104]%31 \equiv -1 \pmod{31}}$,
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Step 7:  which tells us that $x$ must be congruent to $-1$ modulo $31$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Step 8:  To see that, we need $x+4609 \equiv 2104 \pmod{12}$. Since $31 \equiv -1 \pmod{12}$, we note that $x \equiv 1304 \pmod{12}$ and thus $x \equiv -1 \pmod{12}$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Step 9:  We conclude that "x is an . Therefore, the canonical form is "$This number is the least positive integer that satisfies the congruence." Justifying the answer will be helpful for future use. The final answer is:
