[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Yes, let's calculate the length of the common chord in the given scenario.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m gebnue
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m First, we need to understand the geometry of the overlapping circles. Since each circle passes through the center, we can view each circle as a line and visualize the overlapping region at either of the following places:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 1. Either of the circles passes through the center
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 2. Each circle is aligned horizontally and vertically between the centers
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Let's consider the first case first.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We have two circles, each with a radius of 10 cm. This means their centers are 10 cm apart horizontally and 10 cm apart vertically. 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since the common chord is involved, we can trace the mid-point of the horizontal split of the circle which is the center and traces the midpoint between each center circle along its perimeter. So:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - The minimum horizontal length of the chord is given by the vertical distance between the centers minus twice the radius of one circle.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m   \def\sm{\mathrm{i}}\def\emu{\mathrm{m}}\def\fl{\mathrm{f}}\fl$$ \sm=\mathrm{i}(10-\emu)^{-1}+\emu=10\mathrm{i}-\emu^2.$$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - The maximum horizontal length of the chord is the same as the vertical distance between the centers plus twice the radius of one circle.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m   \def\sm{\mathrm{i}}\def\emu{\mathrm{m}}\def\fl{\mathrm{f}}\fl$$ \emu+\mathrm{i}(10-\emu)^{-1}=\emu+(10\mathrm{i}-\emu^2)=-1\mathrm{i}.$$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m So, all we need to focus on is the portion of the chord horizontally aligned with one of the centers. This is the distance between these two radii.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m And, that distance can be actually sketched out as below:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We need to calculate the distance between the centers and the demarcation of one uni-directional center-based length of common chord along it, and noting that:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$B=\frac{C}{\sm}^{2}=98.$$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The formula of "length of the common chord" between the two centers components of the horizontal segment is accordingly given by:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$L_{11}=\frac{1}{2}\left[\text{horizontal segment length between centers of circles minus the horizontal distance of center one}\right].$$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Applying the ~1-variable formula and using direct vector differential properties from Calculus, this yields:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \boxed{L_{11}=9\sqrt{11}.\ L_{12}=4\sqrt{11}.}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m uristic
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m rinse system
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Here's the conclusion:
