[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We can simplify this expression using the properties of powers and roots. 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $ \begin{align*} i^{-100}+i^{-99}+i^{-98}+\cdots+i^{-1}+i^0+i^1+\cdots+i^{99}+i^{100} &= i^{-1}+i^{-=-1}+i^{-=1}+\cdots+i^0+i^1+\cdots+i^{99}+i^{100} \\ &= \frac{1+i+1-i}{1+i^2}+ \frac{1+i^2+1-i^2}{1+i^4}+ \cdots + \frac{1+i^{99}+1-i^{99}}{1+i^{100}} \\ &= \frac{2}{2\cdot 2} + \frac{2}{2\cdot 2\cdot3}+ \frac{2}{2\cdot 2\cdot3\cdot 5}+\cdots+ \frac{2}{2\cdot 2\cdot3\cdot 5\cdots 49}+ \frac{2}{2\cdot 2\cdot3\cdots 49\cdot 50}\end{align*}$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $\textbf{Transfer the idea to 11 terms using the product and 10th product formula.}$ 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $ = \frac{2}{2\cdot 2} + \frac{2}{2\cdot 2\cdot 3}+ \frac{2}{2\cdot 2\cdot 3\cdot 5}+\cdots+ \frac{2}{2\cdot 2\cdot 3\cdot 5\cdots 49}+ \frac{2}{2\cdot 2\cdot 3\cdot 5\cdots 49\cdot 50} \times \frac{2}{2^2} \times \frac{2}{2^2\cdot 3} \times \frac{2}{2^2\cdot 3\cdot 5} \times \cdots \langle 7 \text{ of " "} \text{ products left} \rangle \\ = 2 \times (\frac{1}{4} \times \frac{1}{90})^9 + (\frac{1}{4\cdot 90} \times \frac{1}{90})^9 \times (\frac{1}{4^2\cdot 90^2} \times \frac{1}{90})^9+\cdots \times (\frac{1}{4^9}\cdot\frac{1}{90^9})^9 \\ = 1.71 \text{ x } 10^{9}\text{th row of Pascal's triangle }\langle  \text{count the number of Greeks below this row with 298 as } n\text{ }\rangle$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $\textbf{Express the expression through a sum of 2 or more terms in the form } \sum_{n=0}^{297}a_nT_n. $ 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The right-hand side may be written as  
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $=(1)+\frac{1}{2}+2\cdot\frac{1}{3}+\cdots+296\cdot\frac{1}{49}\cdot 4.$ 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $\textbf{The left-hand side is a geometric series.}$ 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The answer is: \frac{2 \times (1+296)^{49}+1}{10^{9}+1}+\frac{2}{1}<|endoftext|>
