[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Find the sum of the slope and $y$-intercept of the line through the points $(7,8)$ and $(9,0)$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The slope of the line is: $(y_2-y_1)/(x_2-x_1) = (0-8)/(9-7) = -\dfrac{8}{2} = -4$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Therefore, the equation of the line is: $y-8 = -4(x-7)$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Substituting in $y$-intercept point $(7,8)$ yields: $8-8 = -4(7-7)$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m This holds true for any line.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $\blacksquareblingsssundo the equation: $y = -4(x+7) -8$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Explanation:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 1. The equation given is $y = -4(x+7) -8$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 2. To affect the constant on the right side of the equals sign, we must compensate for it on the left side. Here, we need to find the missing coefficient by moving the constant term over to the other side of the equals sign.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    3. To do this, we subtract $-8$ from both sides of the equation: $$\begin{eqnarray} y - (-8) = -4(x + 7) \\\ y + 8 = -4(x + 7) \end{eqnarray}$$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    4. This new equation still has the constant $8$ on the right side.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    5. To eliminate this constant from the equation, we need to move the $-8$ over to the other side (i.e., add $8$ to both sides of the equation): $$\begin{eqnarray} y + 8 + 8 &= -4(x + 7) + 8 \\\ y + 16 &= -4(x + 7) + 8 \end{eqnarray}$$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    6. Distribute the $-4$ on the right-hand side of the equation: $$\begin{eqnarray} y + 16 &= -4x - 28 + 8 \end{eqnarray}$$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    7. Combine like terms on the right-hand side: $$\begin{eqnarray} y + 16 &= -4x - 20 \end{eqnarray}$$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    8. Finally, to find the slope and $y$-intercept, we will use $y$-value of the first point $(7,8)$: $$\begin{align} \text{slope} &= \dfrac{\text{rise}}{\text{run}} = \dfrac{8-8}{7-7} = 0 \end{align}$$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    9. Using $y=mx+b$ to represent the equation of the line, we find the $y$-intercept by setting $x=0$. Thus, the $y$-intercept is $0$.<|endoftext|>
