[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To compute the sum of \( e^{2 \pi i / 13} + e^{4 \pi i / 13} + e^{6 \pi i / 13} + \dots + e^{24 \pi i / 13} \), we recognize that this is a geometric series with the first term \( a = e^{2 \pi i / 13} \) and the common ratio \( r = e^{-2 \pi i / 13} \) (since each term is twice as many complex powers as the previous term).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The sum of the first \( n \) terms of a geometric series is given by the formula:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ S_n = a \frac{1 - r^n}{1 - r}. \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m In this case, \( a = e^{2 \pi i / 13} \), \( r = e^{-2 \pi i / 13} \), and \( n = 13 \). Plugging these values into the formula, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ S_{13} = e^{2 \pi i / 13} \frac{1 - (e^{-2 \pi i / 13})^{13}}{1 - e^{-2 \pi i / 13}}. \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m This further simplifies to:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ S_{13} = e^{2 \pi i / 13} \frac{1 - e^{-26 \pi i / 13}}{1 - e^{-2 \pi i / 13}}. \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Notice that \( e^{-26 \pi i / 13} = (e^{-2 \pi i / 13})^{13} \) and \( e^{-2 \pi i / 13} \neq 1 \). Therefore, \( 1 - e^{-26 \pi i / 13} \neq 1 - e^{-2 \pi i / 13} \). Thus, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ S_{13} = e^{2 \pi i / 13} \frac{1 - e^{-2 \pi i / 13}}{1 - e^{-2 \pi i / 13}} = e^{2 \pi i / 13}. \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m So, the sum of the series is \( e^{2 \pi i / 13} = \cos\left(\frac{2 \pi}{13}\right) + i \sin\left(\frac{2 \pi}{13}\right) \).<|endoftext|>
