[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 1. Let's start by finding a three-digit palindrome that has three digits. Let's denote the first and the middle digits by $a = \_ \_ \_$. To form a three-digit palindrome, we add the middle digits to $a$. The number becomes $111a\_{\_}$, where $a$ is the second and third digits. This means that if we are looking for a (3,3) three-digit palindrome, we have $222a = 222a_{\_}$, which simplifies to $111a = 111a_{\_}$, or $a = 1$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m But let's double-check one more time, by writing two forward-looking numbers to form two from the opposite end. If we use $100$ as the middle digit stays on the same side, it becomes a double, similar to a -111, i.e., $111\_{1}$, which implies $1001 = 1001$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
