[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Yes, I understand. The number of perfect squares between 200 and 300 is 5. Here is the detailed solution:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To find the number of perfect squares between 200 and 300, we need to first identify which numbers in the given range (200 to 300) are perfect squares.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Perfect squares are numbers which can be obtained by squaring integer values. 2, 4, 8, 9, 16, 25, 27, 49, 64 ...
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m In the range 200-300, the perfect squares can be identified as follows:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
