[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m One way is to set up a diagonalization problem.  Since they are perpendicular, their respective ______ matrices will be orthogonal.  We'll write and solve the equation $D_1D_2=D_2D_1$, in which the \textbf{upper}\ text{upper} (and the \textbf{trivial}) entries vanish.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Now let's declare:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $\mathbf{D_1}\displaystyle\begin{pmatrix}3\\-7\end{pmatrix}=y\displaystyle \begin{pmatrix}1\\-2\end{pmatrix},$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $\mathbf{D_2}\displaystyle\begin{pmatrix}a\\2\end{pmatrix}=y\displaystyle \begin{pmatrix}1\\2\end{pmatrix},$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m where, the proper two lines be the unit vector \mathbf{e_1}=(1,0) in 12d and the unit vector \mathbf{e_2}=(3,7) in 12d.  We still have to figure out y.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We can get that
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $\begin{pmatrix}3\\-7\end{pmatrix}=(d_1,-2d_2)^{\textrm{T}},$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m and
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $\begin{pmatrix}a\\2\end{pmatrix}=(r_1,2r_2)^{\textrm{T}},$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m because $\mathbf{D_1}$ and $\mathbf{D_2}$ are diagonal.  Thus, we get
