[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To find the value of $\frac{5! \cdot 2!}{3!}$, we can use the definition of factorial. 5! can be thought of as $5 \cdot 4 \cdot 3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1$ and 3! can be thought of as $3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1$. 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The formula for $\frac {n!}{(n-r)!}$ is Setting $a = n - r$. Simple combinatorics show that this equals to $n \cdot (n-1) \cdot (n-2) \cdot \ldots \cdot (n-r+1)$. 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Therefore, $\frac {5!}{(3-2)!} = \frac {5 \cdot 4 \cdot 3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1}{1} = 5 \cdot 4 \cdot 3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1$. Now I multiply it out, multiplying 5 and 4, then multiply the result by 3, then multiply it by 2, then multiply it by 1. The answer is $5 \cdot 4 \cdot 3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1 = 120$.<|endoftext|>
