[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Mandy has three $20 bills, and she spends $23 each day walking to and from school. How many days will it take for her to have enough money to buy a Magic Kingdom ticket, which costs $120?
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To determine how many days it will take for Mandy to have enough money to buy a Magic Kingdom ticket, we start by calculating how much money she currently has.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Mandy has three $20 bills:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ 3 \times 20 = 60 \text{ dollars} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Next, we need to figure out how much money she has left after spending $23 each day for three days. If she spent $23 on the first two days, then she has:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ 60 - 23 = 37 \text{ dollars left} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Now, we need to determine how many days it will take for her to have the remaining $37. Since she spends $23 each day, we divide the remaining amount by $23:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ 37 \div 23 = 1.538461538 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since Mandy cannot spend a fraction of a day, we round up to the next whole number. Therefore, it will take her 2 days to have enough money to buy the ticket.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m So, the number of days it will take for Mandy to have enough money is:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \boxed{2}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
