[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To solve this problem, we need to compare the volume of water each set of pipes can hold to that of one pipe of the same height with an inside diameter of 12 inches. Let's start by calculating the volume of water that both pipes can hold.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 1. **Calculate the volume of a single pipe of the same height with an inside diameter of 2 inches:**
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m     - Radius (\( r \)) = Diameter / 2 = 2 inches / 2 = 1 inch
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m     - Volume of any cylinder = \(\pi r^2 h\), where \( h \) is the height of the cylinder
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m     - \( h = 4 \) feet = 48 inches
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m     - Volume of the 2-inch pipe = \(\pi \times 1 \times 48 = 192\pi\) cubic inches
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 2. **Calculate the volume of a single pipe of the same height with an inside diameter of 12 inches:**
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m     - Radius (\( r \)) = Diameter / 2 = 12 inches / 2 = 6 inches
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m     - Volume of any cylinder = \(\pi r^2 h\)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m     - \( h = 4 \) feet = 48 inches
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m     - Volume of the 12-inch pipe = \(\pi \times 6 \times 48 = 288\pi\) cubic inches
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 3. **Compare the volumes:**
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m     - The volume of the 12-inch pipe is \( 288\pi \)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m     - The volume of the 2-inch pipe is \( 192\pi \)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 4. **Calculate the number of 4-foot tall pipes needed if the 12-inch pipe fills the same volume as one 12-foot tall pipe of the same height:**
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m     - 4 feet = 48 inches
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m     - Volume required for one 12-foot pipe = \(\pi \times 6 \times 48 = 288\pi\) cubic inches
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m     - Number of 4-foot pipes needed = \( \frac{288\pi}{288\pi} = 1 \)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m So, only one 4-foot tall cylindrical pipe with an inside diameter of 2 inches is needed to hold the same amount of water as one pipe of the same height with an inside diameter of 12 inches.
