[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m At Frank's Fruit Market, 3 bananas cost as much as 2 apples, and 6 apples cost as much as 4 oranges.  How many oranges cost as much as 18 bananas?
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To solve this problem, we need to use the principle of cross-multiplication:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 1. Let's denote the cost of one banana as (bananas). 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 2. We know the relationship between cost and fruits. Since 3 bananas cost as much as 2 apples:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    3 \text{ bananas} \times \text{cost per banana} = 2 \text{ apples}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 3. We also know that since 6 apples cost as much as 4 oranges:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    6 \text{ apples} \times \text{cost per apple} = 4 \text{ oranges}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 4. Come up with an expression for the cost of one apple in terms of bananas:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \text{cost of one apple} = \frac{1}{2} \times \text{cost of one banana}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 5. Substitute the expression for the cost of one apple:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    6 \text{ apples} \times \frac{1}{2} \times \text{cost of one banana} = 4 \text{ oranges}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 6. Match this with the original relationships between bananas and oranges:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    4 \text{ oranges} = \frac{1}{2} \times 2 \text{ apples}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    This tells you that 1 apple can give (2/2) oranges. Therefore:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    1 \text{ apple} = \frac{1}{1} \times 4 \text{ oranges} = 4 \text{ oranges}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 7. Now we find the cost of 18 bananas:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \text{cost of 18 bananas} = 18 \text{ bananas} \times \text{cost per banana}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 8. Multiply:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    18 \text{ bananas} \times \frac{1}{2} \text{ bananas} = 9 \text{ oranges} \times \frac{1}{1} \text{ oranges} = 9 \text{ oranges}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 9. Hence, if (9 oranges) can be represented by (4 apples), then it can be considered as (6 bananas).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \boxed{9}<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
