[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m In a certain amount of time, a plane traveling at a constant $250$ miles per hour traveled $20,\!000$ feet.  In this same amount of time, how many feet would a plane traveling at a constant $400$ miles per hour travel?
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To determine how far a plane would travel in the same time, we need to calculate the distance traveled by the first plane in feet under its initial conditions.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Given:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - The speed of the plane is \( 250 \) miles per hour.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m - The plane travels \( 20,000 \) feet in that time.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We will first convert \( 20,000 \) feet to miles and hours, since the speed is given in miles per hour.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 1. **Convert \( 20,000 \) feet to miles:**
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    1 \text{ mile} = 5280 \text{ feet}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    20,000 \text{ feet} \times \frac{5280 \text{ feet}}{1 \text{ mile}} = 10760,000 \text{ feet}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 2. **Calculate the time \( t \) in hours:**
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \text{Speed} = \text{Distance} / \text{Time}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    20,000 \text{ feet} / 250 \text{ miles per hour} = 80 \text{ hours}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 3. **Calculate the distance traveled by the first plane in feet:**
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    The plane travels a distance of \( 80 \) hours at \( 20,000 \) feet.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \text{Distance} = 20,000 \text{ feet per hour} \times 80 \text{ hours}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \text{Distance} = 160,000 \text{ feet}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Now, let's consider the scenario with the second plane traveling at a constant \( 400 \) miles per hour.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 1. **Convert \( 160,000 \) feet to miles:**
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    160,000 \text{ feet} \times \frac{5280 \text{ feet}}{1 \text{ mile}} = 844,800,000 \text{ feet}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 2. **Calculate the time \( t \) in hours:**
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \text{Speed} = \text{Distance} / \text{Time}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    \[
