[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To find the smallest positive integer that ends in 9 and is divisible by 7, we need to identify the smallest number that meets these criteria, both conditions being that it is divisible by 7.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Let's understand this backwards:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 1. A number that ends in 9 and is divisible by 7 must be a multiple of both 7 and 9, since 7 and 9 are coprime (they share no common factors other than 1).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We can set up our search systematically:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Step 1: Start with the smallest number that ends in 9. This is 19.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Step 2: Find the next number that is divisible by both 7 and 9, since they do not share any common factors. We can check:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    - 29 is divisible by 7 because \(29 \div 7 = 4.142857 \ldots \approx 4 \, \text{with a remainder}\), but we can verify 29 is indeed divisible by both since \(29 \times 7 = 19 \times 7 = 133\), which is divisible by 9 (since \(133 \div 9 = 14 \, \text{with a remainder}\))
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m    - Therefore, 29 is divisible by both 7 and 9.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since 29 meets all our criteria:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Thus, the smallest positive integer that ends in 9 and is divisible by 7 is \(\boxed{29}\).<|endoftext|>
