[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To find the domain of the rational function \( g(x) = \frac{x^3-2x^2+4x+3}{x^2-4x+3} \), we need to determine where the denominator is non-zero. The rational function is undefined when the denominator is zero.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m First, let's set the denominator equal to zero and solve for \( x \):
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ x^2 - 4x + 3 = 0 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m This is a quadratic equation. We can factor it as follows:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ x^2 - 4x + 3 = (x - 1)(x - 3) \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m So, the equation becomes:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ (x - 1)(x - 3) = 0 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Setting each factor equal to zero gives us:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ x - 1 = 0 \quad \text{or} \quad x - 3 = 0 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ x = 1 \quad \text{or} \quad x = 3 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The expressions \( x = 1 \) and \( x = 3 \) are not within the domain of the original function, \( g(x) \). Therefore, the domain of \( g(x) \) is all real numbers except \( x = 1 \) and \( x = 3 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Expressing the domain in interval notation, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ \boxed{(-\infty, 1) \cup (1, 3) \cup (3, \infty)} \]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m (-\infty, 1) \cup (1, 3) \cup (3, \infty)
