[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Adjusting $e^{11 \pi \cdot i / 2}$ into $e^{\deleted{11\pi}i}$ (to a simpler polynomial called a Taylor polynomial) is really just about the same as what you already did earlier.  That is, one just has to divide $e^{11\pi i/2}$ into the Taylor series about $11\pi i/2$ to end up with just a single polynomial in $z$.  In order to insert something like $z=11\pi i/2$, one must solve the equation $f(z)=0$ with $f(z)=e^{11\pi i/2}$, since this is where the connection will lie for us.  Note that the anti-derivative of a function of the form $f(z)=e^{iz}$ is  $\tan \frac{i z}{2}+C$, except for the constant type functions defined as the fib's. The Fib construction is based on manipulating the ordinary unit circle with the tangent function and the improper logarithm function (namely, their sum or minimum). A Fib can be defined with a complex number and would be a geometric curve of the form $F[\zeta]=\tan[\zeta]+\frac{1}{\tan\zeta}+\beta$ if one defined $z=k\cdot\operatorname{Re}(\zeta)+j\operatorname{Im}(\zeta)$ and $C=\sum_\zeta{n\cdot\operatorname{Re}(\zeta)-2\cdot\operatorname{Im}(\zeta)}$ (where $j^2=-1$, $\lvert k\rvert=1$, and $\operatorname{Re}(z)=k\cdot\operatorname{Re}(\zeta)+j\operatorname{Im}(\zeta)$).
