[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m In order to find the product of the least common multiple and the greatest common factor of 20 and 90, we first need to find the greatest common factor and then find the least common multiple.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 1] Find the GCF of 20 and 90.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The possible common factors of 20 and 90 are $$1, 2, 5, 10,$$ and $$20$$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Among them, the greatest common factors of 20 and 90 are $$20.$$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 2] Find the LCM of 20 and 90.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m As already stated above, the LCM is equal to the product of the two numbers.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Therefore, the LCM of 20 and 90 is $20\times 90=1900$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 3] Now, use the values of the LCM and GCF to find the product of the least common multiple and the greatest common factor.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $1900\times 20=38000$.
