[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To find the remainder when 5462 is divided by 9, let's use the process of repeated subtraction in reverse.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We will subtrae 3 from 5462, 3 from 5448, and so on, until we cannot find any more numbers that can be subtracted without going negative. 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We can notice that when $5462$ is divided by $1$, $9$, $99$, $999$, and so on (right previous row), we obtain the remainders $2,2,2,2,2,2$, respectively.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Continuing on this pattern, when we subtract the first number in the right previous row from both the divisor and dividend, the remainder increases by $4$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Therefore, the maximum number that can be drawn indefinitely is $5462\div 9=606\overline{6}$, and the minimum number is $5462\div 3=1820\overline{0}$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m By repeatedly subtrating $3$ from $5462$ until we cannot subtract it without it becoming negative, we find that the solution has a remainders of $-499, -500, -501, ...$, and values which have a remainder of $1$ before repeating.  The remainder can never ever be greater than $9$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Therefore, when $5462$ is divided by $9$, the remainder is $\boxed{1}$.GRESystem<|endoftext|>
