[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $\mathbf{First \;} $ a high school has $6$5 $8$6 wheel races in basketball league. Each race results in one winner, and whoever wins all $6$5 races first oldest gets to participate in Cup events. Each $8$6 that does not result in one winner gets moved to second place of that race. How many different orderings of participants are possible?  Interesting problem!
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m There are two possibilities for ordering participants in Cup races:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 1. Participants start sorrounded by winners.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 2. Participants start out-paranorized by other participants.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m If event 1 happens, The first $6$5 races results in $6$5 possible orderings with no restrictions. For example, both $8$6's can win before the first $6$6. Therefore, there are $6$5 opportunities to start as the $1$st $6$6 winner. In this type of scenario, there are $6$5! ways to arrange the races themselves.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Similarly, if event 2 occurs, Participants can only win 2 of the races ($8$6, $1$ first). The number of ways to arrange every second race is still $6$5!, since each of the $6$5 races is indeed a possible race. Therefore there are $6$5! \cdot 2! ways to arrange the races themselves. 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Hence, the total number of possible ways to arrange participants into Cup races is:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m $$60$$
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Naturally, the sequence of such possibilities would be:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m participants eventually start as first $66$ winner. Start second, first positions in cup races with each second $6$'s that result in second $6$s winning. And finally start third loser, last position allowed only supports once. Therefore, there are $50$ desired ways.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
