[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 1. \(9 = 9^2 = 81\) (palindrome)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 2. \(16 = 4^2 = 16\) (palindrome)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 3. \(36 = 6^2 = 36\) (palindrome)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 4. \(49 = 7^2 = 49\) (palindrome)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 5. \(64 = 8^2 = 64\) (palindrome)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 6. \(81 = 9^2 = 81\) (palindrome)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m There are 6 such perfect squares. Therefore, the final answer is:
