[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m A pipe with inside diameter 10'' is to carry water from a reservoir to a small town in an arid land. Neglecting the friction and turbulence of the water against the inside of the pipes, what is the minimum number of 2''-inside-diameter pipes of the same length needed to carry the same volume of water to the arid town?
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Yes, let's come up with a solution step by step:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 1. First, we want to make sure we understand the concept of "water". 10""" is 83,332 liters. 83332 liters is how many Liters Catharsis contains in a 14 inch long pipe. Mild water temperature of -4 C is 4,165 liters. So kept in one the same pipe is 100 liters.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 2. Since weight of 100 liters water is 6620 gram, the water will be stored in a size 14 inch pipe laying 100 liters/14 inch=i.071", long and will take up Lcm*Ch. = 44*0.071=3.01" length. Due to friction, width of pipe should be strictly half the width length. so width="0.5*"
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 3. To fit catharsis into one, we need 3.01" long pipe so the volume of water in 3.01" long pipe \/3 = 6620/3 = 2207.33 liters of water
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 4. Per other water don't head to the same water in that pipe but walking from other pipe with same end of 15" length as a butt 3.01" tube distribution.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 5. Volume of water given will flow into all 3.01".
