[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m What number two numbers must have in common to fulfill $a \times n = b\times n$ for all positive integers? Assume $n>0$.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To determine the number of common divisors of two numbers \(a\) and \(b\) that can be expressed as \(n\) for all positive integers \(n\), we start by considering the given equation \(a \times n = b \times n\) for all positive integers \(n\).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m First, rearrange the equation to find the condition for \(n\):
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ a = b. \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m This equation shows that \(a\) and \(b\) are equal. However, this only implies that \(a\) and \(b\) are both even (since the product of two even numbers is even).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since \(a\) and \(b\) are both even, the only number \(n\) that satisfies \(n > 0\) for all \(n\) when \(n = 1\) is \(n = 1\). Therefore, the only common divisors are \(1\) and \(a = b\).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Thus, the number of common divisors of \(a\) and \(b\) is \(\boxed{2}\).<|endoftext|>
