[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m For what value of the constant $a$ does the system of equations below have infinitely many solutions?   \begin{align*} 3x + 2y &= 8,\\ 6x &= 2a - 7 - 4y \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To solve the given system of equations:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 3x + 2y &= 8,\\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 6x &= 2a - 7 - 4y
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m we start by expressing the second equation in a more convenient form. We can rewrite the second equation as:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 6x - 2a + 7 = 4y
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Now, we have a system with two equations and two variables. To find the constant $a$, we can substitute one of the original equations into the other. Let's substitute the first equation $3x + 2y = 8$ into the second equation:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 6(8) - 2a + 7 = 4y \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 48 - 2a + 7 = 4y \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 55 - 2a = 4y
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Next, we solve for $y$:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 4y &= 55 - 2a \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m y &= \frac{55 - 2a}{4}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Now, substitute the expression for $y$ back into the original first equation to solve for $x$:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 3x + 2\left(\frac{55 - 2a}{4}\right) &= 8 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 3x + \frac{110 - 4a}{4} &= 8 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 3x + \frac{55 - 2a}{2} &= 8 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \frac{6x}{2} + \frac{55 - 2a}{2} &= 8 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \frac{6x + 55 - 2a}{2} &= 8 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 6x + 55 - 2a &= 16 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 6x = 16 - 55 + 2a \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 6x = 20 + 2a \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m x &= \frac{20 + 2a}{6} \\
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m x &= \frac{10 + a}{3}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Thus, the system has infinitely many solutions if and only if $a = 10$. 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m So, the value of $a$ that makes the system have infinitely many solutions is:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \boxed{10}
