[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To find the values of \( k \) that satisfy the equation \(\cos^2 (k^2 + 6^2)^\circ = 1\), we start by noting that the cosine function equals 1 when its argument is an integer multiple of \(2\pi\). Therefore, we need to find \( k \) such that:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ k^2 + 6^2 = 2n\pi \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m for some integer \( n \geq 0 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m This can be rearranged as:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ k^2 + 36 = 2n\pi \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m or equivalently:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ k^2 = 2n\pi - 36 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We will now identify the possible values of \( 2n\pi - 36 \) for integer solutions \( n \).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m ### Analyzing \( 2n\pi - 36 = 180^\circ \):
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ 2n\pi = 208^\circ \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ n = \frac{208^\circ}{2\pi} \]
