[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Suppose that all four of the numbers \[2 - \sqrt{5}, \;4+\sqrt{10}, \;14 - 2\sqrt{7}, \;-\sqrt{2}\]are roots of the same nonzero polynomial with rational coefficients. What is the smallest possible degree of the polynomial?
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Let's consider the polynomial with roots \(2 - \sqrt{5}\), \(4 + \sqrt{10}\), \(14 - 2\sqrt{7}\), and \(-\sqrt{2}\). Since the polynomial has rational coefficients, if \(2 - \sqrt{5}\) and \(4 + \sqrt{10}\) are roots, then their conjugates \(2 + \sqrt{5}\) and \(4 - \sqrt{10}\) must also be roots. Likewise, \(14 - 2\sqrt{7}\) and \(-\sqrt{2}\) must be roots. We need to determine the smallest possible degree of the polynomial.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 1. Calculate the product of the conjugate roots:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m (2 - \sqrt{5})(2 + \sqrt{5})(4 + \sqrt{10})(4 - \sqrt{10})
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We have the product of squares:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m (2 - \sqrt{5})(2 + \sqrt{5}) = 4 - 5 = -1
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m And the product of the conjugates:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m (4 + \sqrt{10})(4 - \sqrt{10}) = 4^2 - (\sqrt{10})^2 = 16 - 10 = 6
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m So:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m (2 - \sqrt{5})(2 + \sqrt{5})(6) = 6(-1) = -6
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 2. The product of all roots (taking the signs into account) is the constant term divided by the leading coefficient:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \text{The product of the roots is reduced.}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m (-6) = \frac{a_3 a_2 a_1}{\text{Leading coefficient}}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since the product of the roots is -6 (the signs are reduced by a factor of 2, but we need a factor to ensure the largest degree is the one we seek), we focus on the factors involving leading coefficients.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 3. We can find the factors involved in the polynomial by considering the product of:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m (2 - \sqrt{5})(4 + \sqrt{10})(14 - 2\sqrt{7})(-\sqrt{2})
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Now compute the product:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m = 2 \times (-\sqrt{2}) \times 2 \times \sqrt{7} \times 4 \times 10 \times 14
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We can minimize this product by finding the common factors:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m (2 \times 4) \times \left( \frac{1}{2 \times 3} \times \frac{1}{2 \times 3} \times 10 \times \sqrt{7} \times (4 \times 14 + 2 \times 4)\right)
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The leading integer factor will be the least common multiple of these products:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m = -2
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m So the polynomial has degree 4 with factors:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m (x + (2 - \sqrt{5}))(x + (4 + \sqrt{10}))(x + (14 - 2\sqrt{7}))(x + (-\sqrt{2}))
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 4. We see that we must include leading and trailing factors of -1 and -2 to eliminate all rational factors of the form \(\pm 1\), \(\pm \sqrt{4.5}, \pm \sqrt{7} - \sqrt{5},\) etc.
