[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Let's start by using the properties of exponents:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 1. Since \([a + b] = a + a = 2a\) (since \(a\) and \(b\) are complex conjugates and their sum will have even terms), we can simplify the product.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 2. Similarly, for complex roots, \([a^{1/4} + b^{1/4}] = \overline{[a + b]}\), \([b^{1/4} + a^{1/4}] = \overline{[a + b]}\), \([b^{13} + a^{13}] = a^{13} + a^{13}\), etc., since the roots of \(b\) are symmetrical and conjugate to those of \(a\).
