[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To solve this problem, let's assume the angles correspond to 5x, 6x, and 7x degrees. The sum of the angles in a triangle is always 180 degrees. So, we can write the equation:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ 5x + 6x + 7x = 180 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Combine the terms on the left side:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ 18x = 180 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To find \( x \), divide both sides by 18:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ x = \frac{180}{18} = 10 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Now, we can find the measure of each angle by substituting \( x = 10 \) into the expressions for each angle:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ \text{Angle 1} = 5x = 5 \cdot 10 = 50 \, \text{degrees} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ \text{Angle 2} = 6x = 6 \cdot 10 = 60 \, \text{degrees} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ \text{Angle 3} = 7x = 7 \cdot 10 = 70 \, \text{degrees} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Therefore, the number of degrees in the largest of these angles is:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[ \boxed{70} \]<|endoftext|>
