[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m To find the measure of \(\angle 4\) given the angles \(\angle 1 = 76^{\circ}\), \(\angle 2 = 27^{\circ}\), and \(\angle 3 = 17^{\circ}\), we can follow these steps:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 1. Determine the sum of the other three angles in the triangle.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 2. Use the fact that the sum of the interior angles in any triangle is \(180^{\circ}\).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 3. Subtract the sum of \(\angle 1\) and \(\angle 2\) from \(180^{\circ}\) to find \(\angle 4\).
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m First, calculate the sum of \(\angle 1\) and \(\angle 2\):
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \angle 1 + \angle 2 = 76^{\circ} + 27^{\circ} = 103^{\circ}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since the sum of all angles in a triangle is \(180^{\circ}\), we can find \(\angle 4\) by subtracting \(\angle 1\) and \(\angle 2\) from \(180^{\circ}\):
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \angle 4 = 180^{\circ} - \angle 1 - \angle 2 = 180^{\circ} - 103^{\circ} = 77^{\circ}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Therefore, the measure of \(\angle 4\) is \(\boxed{77^{\circ}}\).<|endoftext|>
