[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Thank you for your question. I will perform the necessary calculations to determine the measure of one of the three congruent interior angles.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Let's denote the interior angles of the pentagon as {eq}A {/eq}, {eq}B {/eq}, {eq}C {/eq}, {eq}D {/eq} and {eq}E {/eq}. We know that {eq}A + B + C + D + E = 180^\circ {/eq}. 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Given that {eq}A {/eq} and {eq}B {/eq} are right angles, each {eq}A = 90^\circ {/eq} and {eq}B = 90^\circ {/eq}.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since the pentagon is convex, all interior angles must be within the range {0, 180}.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m We can now consider the case when {eq}C {/eq} and {eq}D {/eq} are congruent, or {eq}C = D {/eq}.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m The sum of the three interior angles {eq}B, C, D {/eq} must equal {eq}180^\circ {/eq} again, giving us:
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m {eq}90^\circ + C + D = 180^\circ {/eq}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m {eq}C + D = 90^\circ {/eq}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m {eq}D = 90^\circ - C {/eq}
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Since {eq}C {/eq} and {eq}D {/eq} are each congruent angles, {eq}C = D = \frac{90^\circ}{2} = 45^\circ {/eq}.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m Therefore, the measure of one of the three congruent interior angles {eq}C {/eq} and {eq}D {/eq} is {eq}45^\circ {/eq}.
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m **Final Answer:**
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1983818)[0m {eq}\boxed{45^\circ} {/eq}<|endoftext|>
